WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:12.400
Hello, everyone, and welcome
to lecture 7 of CME 286.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.000
So today is a very
exciting day because we'll

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.960
be talking about how to evaluate
the quality of the output

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.000
of the text-to-image
generation models

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.760
that we've been seeing so far.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.440
And in particular, if you
think about it, in order

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:32.040
for you to know what
to improve, you first

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.440
need to know how
good your output is.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.800
So that's why this lecture is
going to be quite important.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.320
But before we start,
as usual, we're

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.520
going to recap what
we saw last time.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.040
So if you remember,
last time we focused

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:54.880
on the training lifecycle of
text-to-image generation models.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.560
And we first started
by focusing on the loss

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.640
that we're using for training,
and in particular, we

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:08.480
looked at the time step
t that up until then

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.640
we assume to be drawn from
a uniform distribution.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:17.280
And we saw that it was actually
not fair to treat all time

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.200
steps equally.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.600
And we saw that there
was a distribution that

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.040
was quite nice in
this case, which

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.880
was the logit normal
distribution, which emphasized

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.680
on the middle steps,
which are harder

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.680
to learn compared to the
earlier and the later steps.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.640
And this is mainly because
early on, you have basically

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.740
no information on which for
you to base your prediction,

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:48.600
so you just predict the mean of
where all the observations are.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.640
And then towards the
end, you have almost

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.080
denoised your whole
image, but in the middle,

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.280
you're figuring out where
to go and how you want

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.040
to take those big decisions.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.240
So we not only saw
this, but we also

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:10.520
saw that for a given noise
level, if you have a higher

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:13.960
resolution image,
then you perceived

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:19.400
noise is going to be less than
a lower resolution image, which

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.880
is the reason why you need to
do what we call a timeout step

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.200
shift.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.000
And we saw this formula, which
hopefully is not that haunting

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.760
right now, which
is something that

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.600
is derived by our
uncertainty of what

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:39.400
the underlying pixel value is.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:44.520
And then what we did was go
through the whole training life

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.600
cycle of text-to-image
generation models,

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.960
and we started
with pre-training,

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:54.840
where our goal was to teach our
model how to generate images.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.040
So we saw curriculum
learning, where

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.740
the goal was to teach
our model first,

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:04.600
how to do the easy things
before doing the harder things.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:09.200
And then what we
did was also look

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.760
at how we can handle
different resolutions.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:17.240
And we saw that given that
we have a DIT-based model,

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:19.880
it's only a matter
of having a longer

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.760
input at the end of the day.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:26.760
Then we looked at
post-training approaches

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:32.520
aimed at getting you to
generate more beautiful images.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.680
And here, we saw
two main strategies

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.080
on the supervised side, which
was continued training, focusing

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.720
on acquiring more
knowledge, which

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:47.520
is more relevant to the domain
you want to use your model.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.480
And then supervised
fine tuning, which

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:54.640
is aimed at just improving
the aesthetics of the images

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.040
that you produce.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.720
And we also saw some
preference tuning methods,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:06.760
which were aimed at capturing
negative user signals because up

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:11.440
until then, we only taught
our model what to do,

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:14.240
but not what to not do.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:17.279
So this is the goal of
techniques that are derived

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:21.880
from JRPO, DPO, and so on.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:26.320
And after that, we saw how
we could personalize our text

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:28.680
to image generation model.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:34.600
And we looked at DreamBooth,
which is one such method, which

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:40.080
aims at relying on a
rare token and training

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.920
your model on the
specific object or person

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.600
that you want to
capture, and then

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:49.960
use that tuned model
at inference time

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:55.320
to be able to leverage what you
have learned at training time.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:02.400
And then finally, we saw a range
of distillation methods, which

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:09.200
were aimed at shortening
the number of steps that

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.360
were needed at
inference time, so we

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.140
saw progressive distillation.

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:22.240
We saw distribution matching
distillation, and here we are.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:25.400
Today, we're going to
focus on evaluation,

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.360
which as I mentioned,
is going to be

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:32.440
about assessing how
good of a quality

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:37.320
the output generated image is.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:42.960
So as you can maybe
think, judging

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.240
of the quality of an image
can be quite subjective,

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:54.120
so I want us to start
with a motivating example.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:58.240
And I would like to ask
you, based on an input

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.960
prompt that I'm going to
read, which hopefully,

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:05.360
is familiar by now, whether you
think that image is a good image

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:06.840
or not.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:11.480
So the input prompt is a
teddy bear reading a book.

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:14.360
And here is the generated image.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:17.840
So the question is, do you
think that this generated

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:25.520
image is a good, or bad output?

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:28.240
So who says good?

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:31.240
Who says bad?

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:36.700
We agree it's an output that
is not very pleasing to see,

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:39.480
and the main reason
here is well,

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:41.360
the aesthetics are not great.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.920
You look at the image,
it doesn't look great.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:48.420
OK, so how about this one?

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:56.680
Do you think that this
image is a good image?

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:59.920
Who says it's a good image?

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:01.520
Bad image?

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:05.840
Yeah, so why is it a bad image?

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:09.120
Yeah, so it's indeed not
representing the prompt.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.600
I agree with you,
so it's something

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:13.920
that we call prompt
adherence, where

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:17.840
we want the output to follow
what we have it as input.

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:23.200
And so here, although this
image is great to look at,

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:28.400
well, it's not about a
teddy bear reading a book.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:30.280
Last one.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:35.320
Is this image a
good image or not?

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:39.640
So who says good image?

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:42.000
Bad image?

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:43.780
So why is it a bad image?

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:53.760
So you have a very refined
taste, which is great.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:57.960
So let's suppose that this
images is good enough for us

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:01.380
because here, first it's
following the prompt,

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:03.400
so it's a teddy
bear reading a book.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:08.760
And then second, let's say it's
more aesthetically pleasing

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:11.640
compared to the other ones.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:16.760
So from this small
scale exercise,

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:19.240
what I wanted to point
out was that when

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:25.280
we look at the outputs of a
text-to-image generation model,

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:31.500
we typically look at things with
respect to two main buckets.

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:33.840
Not exhaustive, but
two main buckets.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:39.440
The first one is, if you
take this picture on its own,

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:42.539
does this look like
a good picture?

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:45.560
An aesthetically
pleasing picture?

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:48.440
So here, you think
about whether what

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:50.260
is in there is
physically plausible.

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:53.120
So for instance, if the book
that your teddy bear is reading

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.760
is on the table
and not floating,

00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:01.680
if it's realistic, if a
perceptual quality is there.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:05.280
And then the second
big bucket is

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:11.160
whether the produced output is
indeed following your inputs.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:15.520
So here, it's called
prompt adherence.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:21.020
And what we want to see is
whether the objects, the people,

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:26.320
the locations are indeed
present in the image,

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:30.840
and we want the image
to be of a given style.

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:32.840
And if, for
instance, I say, it's

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:36.580
reading a book about
the CME 296 class,

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:40.080
I want CME 296 to be on
the book, so all of that

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:44.320
would be within that bucket.

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:47.660
But I mentioned that these two
categories are not exhaustive.

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:52.200
Of course, you have a
bunch of other criteria

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:55.720
that you can think of,
so I'll just name a few.

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:57.840
So the first one
is safety, so you

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:01.920
want to make sure you're not
depicting things that are

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:06.120
interpreted in a unsafe way.

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:10.240
You want to make sure that
if you generate something

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:13.280
based on a prompt,
you're not always

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:15.320
generating the same thing,
so you want something

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:18.840
that is a bit diverse.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:22.160
You don't want to memorize
the things that you

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:27.480
have seen as inputs,
and so here, it's

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:30.760
trying to have
some generalization

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:33.840
capabilities for your model.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:35.987
And then you have a bunch
of other dimensions,

00:10:35.987 --> 00:10:37.320
so here, for instance, the bias.

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:40.480
You don't want your
model to always output

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:47.720
the same outputs, which could
be, again, misinterpreted.

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:51.600
So for all these
reasons, what I propose

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:57.280
is that this image
evaluation problem,

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:03.800
we can think of it as us trying
to think if the image that we're

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:09.360
getting as output is
mainly good along these two

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:14.320
dimensions, which are
aesthetics and prompt adherence.

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:18.240
So let's assume that these are
our two main categories that we

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:20.280
care about.

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:23.680
And with that, I
would like to start

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:29.960
with a very simple and natural
way of evaluating your output,

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:34.840
which is to leverage humans.

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:39.440
So here, the idea is
you have your model, you

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:43.640
have some input prompt that
is having your model generate

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:48.860
an image, and what
you're saying is OK,

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:51.080
given this prompt,
given this image,

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:55.400
how would you rate this image?

00:11:55.400 --> 00:12:02.800
So here, one possible option
is to have a scale from 1 to 5,

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:06.880
where 1 is very bad
and 5 is very good.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:13.280
And the idea is to figure out
where your image falls into.

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:17.800
So in this case, let's
assume it's very good.

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:22.200
Then what you would
do is in order

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:27.920
to measure the performance of
your model across a data set,

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:31.060
you would take the
average of the ratings,

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:33.280
so you would do
sum of the ratings,

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:36.800
over the number
of ratings, which

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:39.830
you can call average rating.

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:43.150
So the good thing
with this score

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:47.230
is it is nuanced,
because you have a way

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:51.390
to distinguish a very good image
versus a good image, but not

00:12:51.390 --> 00:12:54.390
a great image and so on.

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:58.470
But the main drawback
there is, given

00:12:58.470 --> 00:13:02.310
that you're letting
more degrees of freedom

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:07.790
for humans to decide on which
rating that is, it can introduce

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:13.910
some noise in the ratings that
you're getting, because humans

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:18.510
may think that an image is a 5,
but someone else can think it's

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:19.010
a 4.

00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:21.830
Someone else can
think it's a three.

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:26.070
So everyone has their own way
of interpreting the scale,

00:13:26.070 --> 00:13:31.230
and so this can introduce
some noise in the ratings.

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:35.330
And also, if you think about
it for a lot of problems,

00:13:35.330 --> 00:13:39.670
it's actually very
hard as a person

00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:41.970
to say if something
is a 4 versus a 5.

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:45.390
It's just a hard problem.

00:13:45.390 --> 00:13:50.350
And so for that
reason, a second method

00:13:50.350 --> 00:13:56.550
of digging into this issue
is instead of having a 1 to 5

00:13:56.550 --> 00:14:01.590
scale, to reduce this problem
into a binary problem, which

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:04.250
is much easier for
humans to handle,

00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:06.430
because here, you
are given an image,

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:10.670
and you just need to say
whether it's good or bad.

00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:16.110
So here, we assume that
the answer is good.

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:20.230
And so the score that we
can think of in this setting

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:22.790
is, again, taking the
sum of the ratings,

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:24.670
over the number of ratings.

00:14:24.670 --> 00:14:28.510
And here, it would
be the pass rate.

00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:31.950
So how many of your
images are what

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:33.510
is the proportion
of your images that

00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:36.390
is actually passing the bar?

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:40.750
So the good thing here
is, it is an easier

00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:46.630
task for humans to
work on, so the ratings

00:14:46.630 --> 00:14:49.590
are less noisy in that sense.

00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:56.470
But one problem is humans,
although it's on a binary scale,

00:14:56.470 --> 00:15:03.510
they have a hard time evaluating
things on an absolute scale.

00:15:03.510 --> 00:15:06.950
So it's easier for
you to, let's say,

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:09.070
compare two things,
as opposed to saying

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:10.910
whether that something
is passing the bar

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:13.490
or not because you need
to have some reference.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:19.990
So for that reason, I want
to propose a third method

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:26.870
of going about doing this,
which is trying to rate

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:32.350
those images by comparing
them to some other images.

00:15:32.350 --> 00:15:36.830
So here, it's the pairwise
comparison setting.

00:15:36.830 --> 00:15:41.870
And the setup would be you
have your input prompts,

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:46.310
and what you do is you
generate two images.

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:50.070
And your task is to say
which one of these images

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:52.950
is better than the other one?

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:56.110
So do you agree with me
that this task is easier

00:15:56.110 --> 00:15:58.890
than the absolute scale task?

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:07.590
So in this case, you agree
that the left one is a better

00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:09.310
image than the right one?

00:16:09.310 --> 00:16:11.990
So I'm glad you said
no for the other one,

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:16.470
because this illustrates the
point that on an absolute scale,

00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:19.630
people have different
expectations.

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:23.470
So they may think that the left
one may not be above the bar,

00:16:23.470 --> 00:16:26.390
but when you compare
these two images,

00:16:26.390 --> 00:16:27.950
it is just obvious
that the left one

00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:30.430
is better than the right one.

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:33.670
And so in that sense, the
ratings that you're getting

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:38.810
are going to be even less noisy
compared to the absolute scale,

00:16:38.810 --> 00:16:42.070
binary one.

00:16:42.070 --> 00:16:45.790
So how about the metric?

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:49.910
So very naively,
a very natural way

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:57.350
would be to just, for a given
model, count the number of wins,

00:16:57.350 --> 00:16:59.470
and divide it by the
number of times it

00:16:59.470 --> 00:17:04.750
was compared to something else.

00:17:04.750 --> 00:17:08.950
So this one is the win rate.

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:11.730
Do you think that's
a good metric?

00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:19.710
So here, what we're
doing is for each model,

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:22.910
we're generating
outputs, and we're

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:24.310
comparing these
outputs to, let's

00:17:24.310 --> 00:17:25.650
say, outputs of another model.

00:17:25.650 --> 00:17:28.910
What we're saying is,
in order to quantify

00:17:28.910 --> 00:17:33.030
the performance of that
first model, the model a,

00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:34.830
what we're doing
is, we're counting

00:17:34.830 --> 00:17:40.470
the number of times images
produced by this model won

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:44.790
over the opponent,
and we divide that

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:47.990
by the number of comparisons.

00:17:47.990 --> 00:17:50.430
So the question is,
do you think that is

00:17:50.430 --> 00:17:52.630
a good way of doing things?

00:17:52.630 --> 00:18:00.190
So the answer is, so for one
reference OK, but for many

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:01.310
not sure.

00:18:01.310 --> 00:18:04.030
Well, you have the
right intuition here.

00:18:04.030 --> 00:18:08.230
So what I want to
say here is winning

00:18:08.230 --> 00:18:11.270
against a model
that is known to be

00:18:11.270 --> 00:18:15.790
excellent is much harder than
winning against a model that

00:18:15.790 --> 00:18:18.670
is known to be bad.

00:18:18.670 --> 00:18:25.590
So our intuition here
is your win proportion

00:18:25.590 --> 00:18:27.530
should not be the
only factor here.

00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:32.030
You should also consider who
you are comparing yourself with,

00:18:32.030 --> 00:18:34.430
and it's exactly
what you mentioned.

00:18:34.430 --> 00:18:37.590
And I'm not sure
if you're tracking

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:40.537
the performance of
text-to-image generation models,

00:18:40.537 --> 00:18:42.370
but typically, you have
something like this.

00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:48.350
You have a leaderboard, where
you have models that are ranked.

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:52.110
And one challenge
with this setting

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:57.630
is that models they come,
but also they can go.

00:18:57.630 --> 00:19:00.670
So when a model
comes, let's suppose

00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:02.750
you want to have
a win rate that is

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:06.910
reflective of the
performance of that model,

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:11.630
you will need to have
the same model be

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:17.190
evaluated against all
models of that list.

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:20.510
And you want all
models of that list

00:19:20.510 --> 00:19:25.950
to be also evaluated against
all other models of that list

00:19:25.950 --> 00:19:29.550
every time the
list gets updated,

00:19:29.550 --> 00:19:32.350
so this is a lot of evaluations.

00:19:32.350 --> 00:19:35.670
So you don't want to always
have to evaluate everyone

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:38.110
against everyone every
time something gets

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:43.150
updated on the list to
make things apple to apple.

00:19:43.150 --> 00:19:48.150
So for that reason, well,
something that we can think of

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:53.390
is to adjust that
win rate metric

00:19:53.390 --> 00:19:59.070
by taking into consideration
how strong your opponent is

00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:01.910
within the metric.

00:20:01.910 --> 00:20:04.350
So how do we want to do that?

00:20:04.350 --> 00:20:08.230
Well, before we go
into exactly how we

00:20:08.230 --> 00:20:10.150
do that, I just want
to illustrate the point

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:11.590
I mentioned.

00:20:11.590 --> 00:20:15.150
So here, let's imagine we have
our model, let's say model A,

00:20:15.150 --> 00:20:19.590
that we want to evaluate
against other models.

00:20:19.590 --> 00:20:25.310
Well, if our model wins
against a bad model,

00:20:25.310 --> 00:20:27.870
your reaction is
OK, and so what?

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:32.530
You already that it's better
than that very basic model,

00:20:32.530 --> 00:20:34.990
so there's nothing to
be surprised about.

00:20:34.990 --> 00:20:39.270
But if the human says
your model is winning

00:20:39.270 --> 00:20:41.650
against another model that
is much, much stronger,

00:20:41.650 --> 00:20:45.750
then your reaction
will be different.

00:20:45.750 --> 00:20:51.630
So the idea is to capture
that within the metric,

00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:54.750
so let's see how
we want to do that.

00:20:54.750 --> 00:21:01.150
So first of all, let's assume
that we have a metric per model

00:21:01.150 --> 00:21:05.670
that we note R. R as in rating.

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:09.070
And let's assume that our model,
model A starts with a rating

00:21:09.070 --> 00:21:12.030
of, let's say, 1,000.

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:15.030
And we're a newcomer,
and in that leaderboard,

00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:17.010
it just happens that
there is a model,

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:23.230
let's suppose it's a bad model,
that has a worse rating, which

00:21:23.230 --> 00:21:26.710
is 600, let's say.

00:21:26.710 --> 00:21:34.750
So let's have our model A be
compared against that bad model.

00:21:34.750 --> 00:21:38.790
How would we want
to update the score?

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:44.110
So one idea could be
to compute the score

00:21:44.110 --> 00:21:50.870
that we think would happen,
if those models that perform

00:21:50.870 --> 00:21:53.830
like they did in the past.

00:21:53.830 --> 00:21:55.730
So let's compute
the expected score,

00:21:55.730 --> 00:22:00.390
and for that, let's just
use this formula, which

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:02.090
gives us the expected score.

00:22:02.090 --> 00:22:04.990
And so here the formula
would be 1 over 1

00:22:04.990 --> 00:22:08.230
plus 10 to the power of
the rating of the model

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:13.550
that you're competing
against, minus your rating,

00:22:13.550 --> 00:22:16.030
over some arbitrary constants.

00:22:16.030 --> 00:22:20.190
And let's assume
that constant is 400.

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:27.350
Then this quantity would be
your expected winning chance.

00:22:27.350 --> 00:22:32.910
And so here, it's 0.9,
so 90% chance of winning.

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:37.750
So now that you have your
baseline, what you want to do

00:22:37.750 --> 00:22:40.170
is compute your actual score.

00:22:40.170 --> 00:22:43.350
So actually, compare
the two models.

00:22:43.350 --> 00:22:47.330
So here, the outcome can
be one of three things,

00:22:47.330 --> 00:22:56.150
so you can either lose, be
tied, or win over the bad model.

00:22:56.150 --> 00:22:59.590
And the idea is to use
these two quantities, so

00:22:59.590 --> 00:23:03.790
the expected score
and the actual score

00:23:03.790 --> 00:23:07.530
to compute your
element of surprise,

00:23:07.530 --> 00:23:11.670
how much you went above,
or how much you went below.

00:23:11.670 --> 00:23:15.670
And here, it would be delta
equal to the actual score

00:23:15.670 --> 00:23:18.750
that you obtained,
minus the score

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:24.910
that you think you would
have obtained on average.

00:23:24.910 --> 00:23:32.870
And here, in this case, if model
A wins, it would be plus 0.1.

00:23:32.870 --> 00:23:37.030
If it loses, then
it's minus 0.9,

00:23:37.030 --> 00:23:42.350
and if there's a tie it's
also a minus, minus 0.4.

00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:47.230
So here, by the way, the numbers
they just come from this formula

00:23:47.230 --> 00:23:49.450
that we just plug
the ratings into that

00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:52.190
and then we compute delta.

00:23:52.190 --> 00:23:54.910
So the good thing
with this observation

00:23:54.910 --> 00:24:01.790
is we see that if we have our
model win against the bad model,

00:24:01.790 --> 00:24:05.990
well, there is not a
lot of gains, which

00:24:05.990 --> 00:24:08.390
is aligned with our intuition.

00:24:08.390 --> 00:24:13.390
And if the model loses, then it
means it's really, really bad,

00:24:13.390 --> 00:24:16.910
so there is a big
downward contribution.

00:24:16.910 --> 00:24:18.990
And even if there
is a tie, we know

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:21.710
that if we're tied
with a very bad model,

00:24:21.710 --> 00:24:26.510
then that's also a bad
contribution for our rating,

00:24:26.510 --> 00:24:32.230
so the first step would be to
just update our rating by adding

00:24:32.230 --> 00:24:35.910
k times delta.

00:24:35.910 --> 00:24:38.510
So that would be the recipe.

00:24:38.510 --> 00:24:44.910
And so the image that I
showed you a few slides ago

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:49.550
was actually this slide, where
I masked the middle columns.

00:24:49.550 --> 00:24:53.130
So this is how the
leaderboards do it.

00:24:53.130 --> 00:24:54.905
In practice, they
compute the score.

00:24:54.905 --> 00:24:56.530
So maybe you may have
heard this score.

00:24:56.530 --> 00:25:00.710
It's called the
Elo rating, and it

00:25:00.710 --> 00:25:05.230
allows you to track the
performance of a model

00:25:05.230 --> 00:25:11.590
by computing the score
as a function of how

00:25:11.590 --> 00:25:13.670
strong your opponent is.

00:25:13.670 --> 00:25:18.630
And this allows you to avoid
having everyone be evaluated

00:25:18.630 --> 00:25:20.630
against everyone
every time there

00:25:20.630 --> 00:25:25.470
is a change in who's
in the leaderboard.

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:27.950
And by the way, Elo
is actually the name

00:25:27.950 --> 00:25:29.890
of the person who came
up with the method.

00:25:29.890 --> 00:25:31.950
It's not an acronym.

00:25:31.950 --> 00:25:33.370
So that's good to note.

00:25:36.070 --> 00:25:39.910
But what are the problems
with this approach?

00:25:39.910 --> 00:25:44.270
Well, every time we want
to evaluate our model,

00:25:44.270 --> 00:25:47.910
if we had to always
involve humans,

00:25:47.910 --> 00:25:50.670
well, it will be a
very expensive task.

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:52.910
So that's the first limitation.

00:25:52.910 --> 00:25:57.630
Also, humans, there's
only 24 hours in a day.

00:25:57.630 --> 00:26:00.270
It's a slow method.

00:26:00.270 --> 00:26:03.215
It can also be
subjective, so the example

00:26:03.215 --> 00:26:04.590
that we had in
the very beginning

00:26:04.590 --> 00:26:06.050
is one example of that.

00:26:06.050 --> 00:26:09.790
Another example can
be try to tell me

00:26:09.790 --> 00:26:11.790
if this image is well lit.

00:26:11.790 --> 00:26:16.370
There are several notions
of something being well lit.

00:26:16.370 --> 00:26:21.150
So again, it's a subjective
task, so answers can differ.

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:25.270
And, of course, humans
are not perfect either.

00:26:25.270 --> 00:26:26.650
Depending on the
time of the day,

00:26:26.650 --> 00:26:28.310
you may have external
circumstances

00:26:28.310 --> 00:26:33.350
that may also influence on
the quality of your ratings.

00:26:33.350 --> 00:26:38.190
So all of that is I
hope, a motivation for us

00:26:38.190 --> 00:26:44.190
to look at automated metrics
that would allow us to quantify

00:26:44.190 --> 00:26:48.190
how good the outputs are.

00:26:48.190 --> 00:26:50.750
And we're going
to start with what

00:26:50.750 --> 00:26:53.270
we call reference-free
metrics, and I'm

00:26:53.270 --> 00:26:57.870
going to tell you in a second
what reference-free means.

00:26:57.870 --> 00:27:04.550
So as I mentioned, we're in the
text to image generation case.

00:27:04.550 --> 00:27:08.590
We have a text as
input and we have

00:27:08.590 --> 00:27:11.550
a model that produces an image.

00:27:11.550 --> 00:27:15.110
So one thing that I want to
tell you is there are they're

00:27:15.110 --> 00:27:17.110
just multiple ways
you can produce

00:27:17.110 --> 00:27:22.230
an image for a same
prompt like these ones.

00:27:22.230 --> 00:27:33.190
And for that reason, having
a single image as reference

00:27:33.190 --> 00:27:36.710
is a little bit unfair
because you would always

00:27:36.710 --> 00:27:40.670
try to compare against
that same reference.

00:27:40.670 --> 00:27:44.510
That is not the only
way you can do things,

00:27:44.510 --> 00:27:48.150
and so for that reasons, the
metrics that we're going to see

00:27:48.150 --> 00:27:50.710
are called reference-free,
as in they're not

00:27:50.710 --> 00:27:55.530
using a single output
reference image as reference.

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:57.910
They're not doing that.

00:27:57.910 --> 00:28:01.390
So that's the reference-free.

00:28:01.390 --> 00:28:05.150
And so let's go
back to our problem.

00:28:05.150 --> 00:28:09.330
And so here, we have an
input, which is a text prompt,

00:28:09.330 --> 00:28:12.710
and then we have
an image as output.

00:28:12.710 --> 00:28:16.990
And I told you we could
consider our problem

00:28:16.990 --> 00:28:22.110
as being us trying to
quantify how good the image is

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:25.150
with respect to aesthetics
and with respect

00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:27.110
to prompt adherence.

00:28:27.110 --> 00:28:31.870
Well, here, let's
start with aesthetics.

00:28:31.870 --> 00:28:34.830
And let's take our
generated image,

00:28:34.830 --> 00:28:39.830
which is on the right and our
real image that is on the left.

00:28:39.830 --> 00:28:41.750
So as I mentioned,
it is not fair

00:28:41.750 --> 00:28:47.230
for us to compare our generated
image with a single reference.

00:28:47.230 --> 00:28:53.990
So one idea can be to actually
take many generated images

00:28:53.990 --> 00:28:57.630
and many real images.

00:28:57.630 --> 00:29:00.870
And instead of comparing
your single generated image

00:29:00.870 --> 00:29:04.590
to the single real
image, one idea

00:29:04.590 --> 00:29:11.550
could be to look at the
distribution of both

00:29:11.550 --> 00:29:15.430
and see how the two compares.

00:29:15.430 --> 00:29:16.870
So let's do that.

00:29:16.870 --> 00:29:18.630
Well, how would you do it?

00:29:18.630 --> 00:29:20.990
Well, in the class,
we've seen that we

00:29:20.990 --> 00:29:26.350
have methods to represent images
in what we call a latent space,

00:29:26.350 --> 00:29:29.230
or some space.

00:29:29.230 --> 00:29:35.430
So here, one idea could be to
take our, let's say, real images

00:29:35.430 --> 00:29:41.150
and have them be inputs
into some encoder such

00:29:41.150 --> 00:29:44.710
that they can be
represented in space.

00:29:44.710 --> 00:29:49.270
So we can do that with
the sets of real images.

00:29:49.270 --> 00:29:53.270
And in parallel, we can also
take our generated images,

00:29:53.270 --> 00:29:58.630
and also let them through
that pre-trained encoder

00:29:58.630 --> 00:30:02.830
so that they can also be
represented in that same space.

00:30:02.830 --> 00:30:06.470
So the idea that
we can have here

00:30:06.470 --> 00:30:11.510
is to try to compare
how these two resulting

00:30:11.510 --> 00:30:17.670
distributions in this space,
how these two compare.

00:30:17.670 --> 00:30:20.690
So in order to characterize
each distribution,

00:30:20.690 --> 00:30:23.950
you can have the mean, mu,
and the covariance matrix

00:30:23.950 --> 00:30:28.030
sigma that can just tell you
about how your distribution is

00:30:28.030 --> 00:30:29.870
shaped.

00:30:29.870 --> 00:30:32.410
And what you want to do
is to compare the two.

00:30:32.410 --> 00:30:34.970
So you want to compare
where they are located,

00:30:34.970 --> 00:30:40.390
so here, you can use the
mean, and how they are shaped,

00:30:40.390 --> 00:30:42.130
you can use the
covariance sigma.

00:30:42.130 --> 00:30:45.630
So the covariance
matrix is a quantity

00:30:45.630 --> 00:30:49.810
that tells you how spread
out your distribution is,

00:30:49.810 --> 00:30:55.770
and also what are the
directions of that spread.

00:30:58.510 --> 00:31:00.710
So by the way, why
would you also want

00:31:00.710 --> 00:31:02.410
to quantify the spread?

00:31:02.410 --> 00:31:08.750
Well, you also want your
generated images to be diverse,

00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:13.150
and so here, the spread will be
around quantifying how diverse

00:31:13.150 --> 00:31:16.350
your generated images are.

00:31:16.350 --> 00:31:19.150
And so, as I
mentioned, what we want

00:31:19.150 --> 00:31:25.230
is to quantify the distance
between these two distributions.

00:31:25.230 --> 00:31:30.510
And so we can come up
with some formula, which

00:31:30.510 --> 00:31:31.730
I'm putting it on the slide.

00:31:31.730 --> 00:31:36.350
And we're going to go
through each component, where

00:31:36.350 --> 00:31:39.350
the first term is
around quantifying

00:31:39.350 --> 00:31:42.890
the difference in where these
distributions are located,

00:31:42.890 --> 00:31:48.030
so comparing the means
of each distribution,

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:53.590
and also comparing the shape
of each of these distributions.

00:31:53.590 --> 00:31:57.830
So here, so Tr stands
for trace, so it's

00:31:57.830 --> 00:32:01.070
an operation on a matrix.

00:32:01.070 --> 00:32:04.510
And maybe it's the first time
you're seeing this formula,

00:32:04.510 --> 00:32:07.598
your first reaction
may be, well,

00:32:07.598 --> 00:32:08.890
where do you have this formula?

00:32:08.890 --> 00:32:11.590
What does it represent?

00:32:11.590 --> 00:32:15.870
So the truth is this metric,
which is called the Fréchet

00:32:15.870 --> 00:32:19.550
Inception Distance,
is a metric that

00:32:19.550 --> 00:32:23.990
is derived from a distance
that is called the Wasserstein

00:32:23.990 --> 00:32:28.910
distance that aims at
quantifying how much effort you

00:32:28.910 --> 00:32:35.270
would have to do to go from one
distribution to another one.

00:32:35.270 --> 00:32:39.030
And it just turns
out that if you

00:32:39.030 --> 00:32:44.750
assume that the
distribution of real images

00:32:44.750 --> 00:32:48.270
and the distribution
of generated images,

00:32:48.270 --> 00:32:50.870
they are each Gaussian.

00:32:50.870 --> 00:32:54.350
If you assume that,
then it just happens

00:32:54.350 --> 00:32:58.270
that this Wasserstein
distance, which

00:32:58.270 --> 00:33:02.270
is something that typically does
not have a closed form solution,

00:33:02.270 --> 00:33:06.430
is something that you can
express in a closed form.

00:33:06.430 --> 00:33:10.190
So that's where the
formula comes from.

00:33:10.190 --> 00:33:12.290
But in terms of
interpreting each term,

00:33:12.290 --> 00:33:16.230
you can think of the first term
as being the difference in terms

00:33:16.230 --> 00:33:21.330
of location, and the second
one is how the shapes differ.

00:33:24.550 --> 00:33:25.370
Cool.

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:28.510
So it's a distance, so what
you want is those distances

00:33:28.510 --> 00:33:30.570
to be as small as possible.

00:33:30.570 --> 00:33:35.870
So the lower your
FID, the better it is.

00:33:35.870 --> 00:33:39.090
So the question is, can
you use VLM to do that?

00:33:39.090 --> 00:33:41.690
So we're going to see that
later in this lecture.

00:33:45.310 --> 00:33:49.950
So the question is, can
you say a little bit

00:33:49.950 --> 00:33:51.790
about how to
interpret these terms

00:33:51.790 --> 00:33:53.970
and how this relates to
these [INAUDIBLE] Gaussians?

00:33:53.970 --> 00:33:56.070
So we're not going
to go into that,

00:33:56.070 --> 00:34:02.470
but the underlying
idea behind this metric

00:34:02.470 --> 00:34:09.510
is to quantify how much effort
you would have to transport

00:34:09.510 --> 00:34:11.909
one distribution to another.

00:34:11.909 --> 00:34:14.389
And this is, you can think
of it as some formula

00:34:14.389 --> 00:34:16.510
that has this interpretation.

00:34:16.510 --> 00:34:21.429
And what I was saying was,
if you assume that the shape,

00:34:21.429 --> 00:34:25.150
or that your real
image distribution,

00:34:25.150 --> 00:34:28.090
and if you assume that your
generated image distribution,

00:34:28.090 --> 00:34:31.830
if you assume that each
of them is Gaussian,

00:34:31.830 --> 00:34:35.429
then what we're saying is that
the quantity that I mentioned

00:34:35.429 --> 00:34:40.110
can actually be computed with
a rather nice formula that

00:34:40.110 --> 00:34:41.790
is closed form.

00:34:41.790 --> 00:34:46.010
So this goes into the broader
theme of Gaussian distributions,

00:34:46.010 --> 00:34:47.989
just simplifying
a lot of things,

00:34:47.989 --> 00:34:50.050
and this is one of
those instances.

00:34:52.949 --> 00:34:55.050
So the question is the
real here is the data?

00:34:55.050 --> 00:35:00.390
Yes, so you would take a set of
real images, so non-generated

00:35:00.390 --> 00:35:05.630
images, and you would
compute their representation

00:35:05.630 --> 00:35:07.630
using the pre-trained encoder.

00:35:07.630 --> 00:35:09.710
And you would use
those representations

00:35:09.710 --> 00:35:14.910
to represent those real images.

00:35:14.910 --> 00:35:17.250
So the question is, can
I use it in pixel space?

00:35:17.250 --> 00:35:19.070
So here's the thing.

00:35:19.070 --> 00:35:23.830
This metric is there for you to
compare how well your model is

00:35:23.830 --> 00:35:26.790
doing compared to
other models out there,

00:35:26.790 --> 00:35:32.110
so you need to have some
space that is the same as what

00:35:32.110 --> 00:35:34.010
the other ones have mentioned.

00:35:34.010 --> 00:35:37.630
And so here this metric is
called Fréchet Inception

00:35:37.630 --> 00:35:41.470
Distance because actually
the pre-trained encoder is

00:35:41.470 --> 00:35:44.550
the Inception network.

00:35:44.550 --> 00:35:47.910
And so what you want is, if
you want to compare yourself

00:35:47.910 --> 00:35:51.910
with someone else,
you necessarily

00:35:51.910 --> 00:35:55.070
need to take the
same representation

00:35:55.070 --> 00:35:57.470
to make things comparable.

00:35:57.470 --> 00:36:00.290
So the question is if your
diffusion model is pixel space,

00:36:00.290 --> 00:36:01.490
is this still applicable?

00:36:01.490 --> 00:36:06.790
Yes, so what you would do is you
have your real images that you

00:36:06.790 --> 00:36:08.270
go through a pre-trained model.

00:36:08.270 --> 00:36:10.810
Your Pixel space
diffusion, no problem.

00:36:10.810 --> 00:36:13.770
You do your diffusion in
pixel space and at the end,

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:17.590
you get those generated
images and you input them

00:36:17.590 --> 00:36:19.210
into that Inception model.

00:36:19.210 --> 00:36:21.010
And then you have
your representation.

00:36:24.110 --> 00:36:24.667
Oh, yeah.

00:36:24.667 --> 00:36:25.250
Good question.

00:36:25.250 --> 00:36:27.670
So the question is,
are these conditioned

00:36:27.670 --> 00:36:28.930
on a particular prompt?

00:36:28.930 --> 00:36:30.430
So it's a great question.

00:36:30.430 --> 00:36:34.350
Well, you touch a
great point, which

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:39.150
is that you want your
model to perform well

00:36:39.150 --> 00:36:40.590
on what you care about.

00:36:40.590 --> 00:36:44.790
So, for instance, you
may care about generating

00:36:44.790 --> 00:36:52.110
faces, let's say, or generating
some nature, some indoor scenes.

00:36:52.110 --> 00:36:56.310
So depending on
what your task is,

00:36:56.310 --> 00:37:01.990
you may want to compare your
generated images with respect

00:37:01.990 --> 00:37:06.550
to a set of real images that
are representative of the thing

00:37:06.550 --> 00:37:07.650
that you care about.

00:37:07.650 --> 00:37:09.190
So you have a
number of data sets

00:37:09.190 --> 00:37:14.230
like ImageNet is
one that's quite--

00:37:14.230 --> 00:37:15.210
it's a classic one.

00:37:15.210 --> 00:37:20.110
And then you have, I
believe, MS COCO, and so on.

00:37:20.110 --> 00:37:23.110
And so you can
think of the images

00:37:23.110 --> 00:37:26.390
that you generate
as being conditioned

00:37:26.390 --> 00:37:29.030
by a set of prompts
that are going

00:37:29.030 --> 00:37:33.470
to match what is there, so it
can be either captions, or class

00:37:33.470 --> 00:37:34.790
conditions.

00:37:34.790 --> 00:37:38.910
So you have something that
allows you to compare yourself

00:37:38.910 --> 00:37:40.830
to that distribution.

00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:43.470
And typically, you
would use a data

00:37:43.470 --> 00:37:46.570
set that is aligned with
your task of interest,

00:37:46.570 --> 00:37:49.190
and that data set would
contain the thing that you

00:37:49.190 --> 00:37:51.650
would use as inputs.

00:37:51.650 --> 00:37:54.530
Great question.

00:37:54.530 --> 00:37:56.010
Great question.

00:37:56.010 --> 00:37:58.420
So the question is,
in practice, how large

00:37:58.420 --> 00:38:00.960
is the set of real
images that you take?

00:38:00.960 --> 00:38:03.860
So typically, 50k.

00:38:03.860 --> 00:38:07.480
And actually, speaking of
that, we've seen in lecture 5,

00:38:07.480 --> 00:38:13.980
I'm not sure if you
remember, we had DIT,

00:38:13.980 --> 00:38:19.080
which measured its performance
on that FID 50k scale.

00:38:19.080 --> 00:38:25.820
So 50k here means that we have
a set of 50,000 real images.

00:38:25.820 --> 00:38:31.060
So here, what you would do is
generate 50,000 generated images

00:38:31.060 --> 00:38:34.460
and compare that distribution
with the set of 50,000 real

00:38:34.460 --> 00:38:36.100
images.

00:38:36.100 --> 00:38:38.840
But I've also seen 30,000.

00:38:38.840 --> 00:38:42.540
Typically, in the order
of tens of thousands.

00:38:42.540 --> 00:38:49.100
So the question is, is it
fair to use variance and mean

00:38:49.100 --> 00:38:50.880
to characterize the
whole distribution?

00:38:50.880 --> 00:38:53.500
Is that roughly your--

00:38:53.500 --> 00:38:54.040
oh yeah.

00:38:54.040 --> 00:38:57.500
So the question is,
is it fair to assume

00:38:57.500 --> 00:39:01.260
that the shape and the distance
is representative of quality?

00:39:01.260 --> 00:39:03.140
So it's a great question.

00:39:03.140 --> 00:39:08.860
Actually, one funny thing is,
if you look at social media,

00:39:08.860 --> 00:39:11.740
I would say, every week, you
would see a new paper coming up

00:39:11.740 --> 00:39:13.980
with a new metric.

00:39:13.980 --> 00:39:17.740
So this metric is by far
not a perfect metric,

00:39:17.740 --> 00:39:23.220
and the community voices
that out, for sure.

00:39:23.220 --> 00:39:24.480
So yeah, I agree with you.

00:39:24.480 --> 00:39:27.340
It is not necessarily
indicative of the quality

00:39:27.340 --> 00:39:30.480
that you want to reach, but
it's a good enough proxy,

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:33.580
or, at least, that's what
the community thinks.

00:39:33.580 --> 00:39:36.340
And I would say, so
it's a personal take.

00:39:36.340 --> 00:39:40.980
One of the reasons why it's
been hard to change the metric

00:39:40.980 --> 00:39:45.140
that people use is because when
you want to publish a paper,

00:39:45.140 --> 00:39:48.780
typically you want to show
that your method is good,

00:39:48.780 --> 00:39:52.420
so you want to compare yourself
to some previous method.

00:39:52.420 --> 00:39:56.020
And its people have
just been using FID.

00:39:56.020 --> 00:40:00.300
So that's why it's really
hard to change from that.

00:40:00.300 --> 00:40:02.580
But I agree with your
point, which it is not

00:40:02.580 --> 00:40:06.860
necessarily reflective of
the quality of your images,

00:40:06.860 --> 00:40:09.140
which is why in
papers, you would also

00:40:09.140 --> 00:40:14.100
see a bunch cherry picked or
not cherry picked images that

00:40:14.100 --> 00:40:18.440
are shown in the paper just
to show how good the model is.

00:40:21.340 --> 00:40:23.100
Cool.

00:40:23.100 --> 00:40:23.600
Great.

00:40:23.600 --> 00:40:25.700
So I'm looking at the time.

00:40:25.700 --> 00:40:33.900
I will move ahead, and
just say that if there's

00:40:33.900 --> 00:40:36.380
a big gap in the
location, you can

00:40:36.380 --> 00:40:40.140
think of it as maybe the quality
or the style being different.

00:40:40.140 --> 00:40:42.540
And when it comes to the
shape being different,

00:40:42.540 --> 00:40:47.380
for instance, the
generated images

00:40:47.380 --> 00:40:51.220
is in a very constrained
region, maybe

00:40:51.220 --> 00:40:55.460
your model is not
generating diverse sets.

00:40:55.460 --> 00:40:58.040
So these are the points
to watch out for.

00:40:58.040 --> 00:41:02.020
And in terms of the sample
size, so as we mentioned,

00:41:02.020 --> 00:41:08.780
the typical reference is
FID-50k of a given data set.

00:41:08.780 --> 00:41:12.340
And, of course, so
we're looking at FID,

00:41:12.340 --> 00:41:16.420
which is part of this section
of reference-free metrics,

00:41:16.420 --> 00:41:19.940
and here we're using a
reference distribution.

00:41:19.940 --> 00:41:23.260
So I know that it
may sound confusing,

00:41:23.260 --> 00:41:26.780
but I just want you to
know that reference-free,

00:41:26.780 --> 00:41:28.260
the title of this
section, refers

00:41:28.260 --> 00:41:34.620
to us not comparing one
generated output with one

00:41:34.620 --> 00:41:36.540
real image.

00:41:36.540 --> 00:41:40.500
It is more about comparing
the distribution.

00:41:40.500 --> 00:41:44.980
And then the last thing is, one
of the main complaints of people

00:41:44.980 --> 00:41:50.860
is that the distribution of real
images and the distribution of

00:41:50.860 --> 00:41:55.300
generated images, they are
typically not Gaussian,

00:41:55.300 --> 00:42:01.220
so this formula is
typically not 100% valid.

00:42:01.220 --> 00:42:04.440
So the question is this
formula only for Gaussians?

00:42:04.440 --> 00:42:06.540
So the formula is
actually derived

00:42:06.540 --> 00:42:11.420
from this other quantity that's
called the Wasserstein distance.

00:42:11.420 --> 00:42:14.220
And so this formula
is specifically

00:42:14.220 --> 00:42:16.740
when you compare two
distributions that

00:42:16.740 --> 00:42:19.220
are Gaussians.

00:42:19.220 --> 00:42:21.700
And so that's
another limitation,

00:42:21.700 --> 00:42:26.700
and a point that you will see
in papers when they're OK,

00:42:26.700 --> 00:42:30.780
FID is not that
great of a metric.

00:42:30.780 --> 00:42:33.500
Cool, so this is the aesthetics.

00:42:33.500 --> 00:42:38.660
So FID is the metric that most
people use, for better or worse,

00:42:38.660 --> 00:42:41.060
to quantify how
good of a quality

00:42:41.060 --> 00:42:43.780
your generated images are.

00:42:43.780 --> 00:42:46.280
And second, as
previously mentioned,

00:42:46.280 --> 00:42:48.860
we're going to look
at prompt adherence.

00:42:48.860 --> 00:42:52.700
How can we, or how
can we quantify

00:42:52.700 --> 00:42:55.540
that the output image
that we're getting

00:42:55.540 --> 00:42:59.780
is aligned with the input?

00:42:59.780 --> 00:43:02.220
And so for this,
actually, you may

00:43:02.220 --> 00:43:10.540
have in mind a method we
saw in lecture 4 CLIP, which

00:43:10.540 --> 00:43:12.580
stands for Contrastive
Language-Image

00:43:12.580 --> 00:43:19.460
Pre-training, which aimed at
comparing the text and an image

00:43:19.460 --> 00:43:23.660
by encoding each separately
through their respective

00:43:23.660 --> 00:43:29.660
encoders and training the
resulting system using

00:43:29.660 --> 00:43:35.420
a contrastive loss that
incentivizes elements that are

00:43:35.420 --> 00:43:38.780
similar to have a higher score,
and elements that are not

00:43:38.780 --> 00:43:42.220
similar to have a lower score.

00:43:42.220 --> 00:43:47.300
So you could very
well use that model,

00:43:47.300 --> 00:43:50.420
where you would put
the input text that you

00:43:50.420 --> 00:43:54.420
use to generate the image
along with the generated image.

00:43:54.420 --> 00:43:56.920
And there is a model out there
that's called the CLIP score.

00:43:56.920 --> 00:43:59.540
That is actually
exactly that CLIP model,

00:43:59.540 --> 00:44:06.580
that allows you to get an actual
score in terms of how aligned

00:44:06.580 --> 00:44:10.860
your text and your image are.

00:44:10.860 --> 00:44:12.700
That's called the CLIP score.

00:44:12.700 --> 00:44:19.460
And speaking of taking into
consideration both the prompt

00:44:19.460 --> 00:44:24.700
and the image, you can
also think of doing so

00:44:24.700 --> 00:44:27.540
using a CLIP like model.

00:44:27.540 --> 00:44:33.020
Not in order to predict how
aligned the two are, but maybe

00:44:33.020 --> 00:44:37.860
to align, or maybe
to predict how good

00:44:37.860 --> 00:44:40.820
the image is to the input text.

00:44:40.820 --> 00:44:44.020
So you could very
well take such a model

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:51.900
and train it on a data set that
is around human preferences,

00:44:51.900 --> 00:44:53.420
and that's what people did.

00:44:53.420 --> 00:44:55.060
So this is the PickScore.

00:44:55.060 --> 00:44:58.740
So what they did was
they took a CLIP model

00:44:58.740 --> 00:45:01.860
and they trained it
on preference data,

00:45:01.860 --> 00:45:07.700
where you had image and text and
the label was the preference.

00:45:07.700 --> 00:45:10.740
And here, you can
get a PickScore,

00:45:10.740 --> 00:45:14.620
which is a holistic
score that combines

00:45:14.620 --> 00:45:18.880
both the aesthetics and
the prompt adherence,

00:45:18.880 --> 00:45:21.100
along with some other
things to tell you

00:45:21.100 --> 00:45:26.180
about the overall satisfaction
of the human with respect

00:45:26.180 --> 00:45:29.860
to the generated output.

00:45:29.860 --> 00:45:35.620
So this is the last metric I
wanted to talk to you about.

00:45:35.620 --> 00:45:40.740
Now, I want us to
take a step back.

00:45:40.740 --> 00:45:47.260
When we talk about evaluation,
we may think about the image

00:45:47.260 --> 00:45:52.180
generation model, but as we saw,
it's not actually the only model

00:45:52.180 --> 00:45:54.500
we're interested in.

00:45:54.500 --> 00:46:00.780
We've also seen other
pieces that do matter.

00:46:00.780 --> 00:46:04.060
For instance, the
VAE, which is tasked

00:46:04.060 --> 00:46:08.180
with reconstructing the input.

00:46:08.180 --> 00:46:15.420
And in this setting, well, your
label is much, much clearer

00:46:15.420 --> 00:46:18.260
because what you want
to do, your proxy task

00:46:18.260 --> 00:46:23.180
is to reconstruct
the original input.

00:46:23.180 --> 00:46:26.860
And so in that sense,
you do have a reference

00:46:26.860 --> 00:46:30.660
to base yourself on,
so you could very well,

00:46:30.660 --> 00:46:35.540
in this setting, want to compare
what you output with what

00:46:35.540 --> 00:46:38.620
you want it to output.

00:46:38.620 --> 00:46:41.400
And this is the reason
why in this section,

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:45.620
we're going to go through such
metrics, which we're calling

00:46:45.620 --> 00:46:49.780
reference-based as
opposed to reference-free,

00:46:49.780 --> 00:46:53.940
because reference-based
metrics have something

00:46:53.940 --> 00:47:00.380
that you can use to compare
your generated image against.

00:47:00.380 --> 00:47:04.660
So I mentioned the VAE, but you
have actually other use cases

00:47:04.660 --> 00:47:05.240
as well.

00:47:05.240 --> 00:47:13.180
So we've not really gone
into details about text image

00:47:13.180 --> 00:47:17.060
to image tasks, so image
editing tasks, but you

00:47:17.060 --> 00:47:22.673
may also want to compare
how your edited image is

00:47:22.673 --> 00:47:24.340
with respect to the
input image, to make

00:47:24.340 --> 00:47:25.757
sure you're
controlling for things

00:47:25.757 --> 00:47:28.240
that you don't want
to edit, and so on.

00:47:28.240 --> 00:47:30.420
So this is not
just something that

00:47:30.420 --> 00:47:35.900
is valid for VAEs, but for
other use cases as well.

00:47:35.900 --> 00:47:41.640
Cool, so just getting a bit more
rigorous about our notations.

00:47:41.640 --> 00:47:45.340
So the input is noted x.

00:47:45.340 --> 00:47:49.220
The outputs, which is an attempt
at reconstructing the input

00:47:49.220 --> 00:47:55.100
is called, or is noted x hat.

00:47:55.100 --> 00:47:59.660
And what you want is to
have a quantified metric

00:47:59.660 --> 00:48:03.100
to compare the two
that hopefully,

00:48:03.100 --> 00:48:08.340
will align with the quality
of your reconstruction.

00:48:08.340 --> 00:48:10.820
So the first metric that
you may think about,

00:48:10.820 --> 00:48:14.820
I mean, the formula looks
scary, but it's just

00:48:14.820 --> 00:48:17.580
about comparing pixels--

00:48:17.580 --> 00:48:20.580
just pixel-wise distance.

00:48:20.580 --> 00:48:25.340
And you may also that distance
under the name MSE, which

00:48:25.340 --> 00:48:28.900
stands for Mean Squared Error.

00:48:28.900 --> 00:48:35.180
And so this metric, what it does
is it takes these two images

00:48:35.180 --> 00:48:41.140
and it just computes the
pixel-wise distance of a given

00:48:41.140 --> 00:48:44.220
pixel in the reference
image and the pixel

00:48:44.220 --> 00:48:46.780
in your generated image.

00:48:46.780 --> 00:48:49.340
And it does that for all pixels.

00:48:49.340 --> 00:48:56.660
And one big drawback is it
is very sensitive to exactly

00:48:56.660 --> 00:48:59.160
the alignment between
these two images,

00:48:59.160 --> 00:49:04.180
and so if you have generated
a perfect reconstruction,

00:49:04.180 --> 00:49:07.760
but that is slightly
shifted a few pixels,

00:49:07.760 --> 00:49:10.580
let's say to the
right, then your MSC

00:49:10.580 --> 00:49:13.060
will be actually terrible.

00:49:13.060 --> 00:49:15.100
So that's one drawback.

00:49:15.100 --> 00:49:20.100
The second drawback is if
we tell you about an MSC,

00:49:20.100 --> 00:49:22.540
let's say it's 10, I don't know.

00:49:22.540 --> 00:49:25.220
Well, you don't what
to do with that number

00:49:25.220 --> 00:49:29.020
because it depends how
you encode your pixels.

00:49:29.020 --> 00:49:31.040
So sometimes it is
between 0 and 1.

00:49:31.040 --> 00:49:34.060
Sometimes it is
between 0 and 255.

00:49:34.060 --> 00:49:40.060
The scale varies,
which is the reason why

00:49:40.060 --> 00:49:42.480
you have another metric
that is still pixel-wise

00:49:42.480 --> 00:49:49.340
that is called the Peak
Signal-to-Noise Ratio, PSNR,

00:49:49.340 --> 00:49:54.100
which normalizes MSE with
respect to the maximum value

00:49:54.100 --> 00:49:56.580
that it can take.

00:49:56.580 --> 00:50:00.220
So this allows you to, at
least, get a reference,

00:50:00.220 --> 00:50:03.660
or put things into context.

00:50:03.660 --> 00:50:07.460
So it not only does that,
it also wraps the thing

00:50:07.460 --> 00:50:10.300
into a logarithm.

00:50:10.300 --> 00:50:14.580
And I just want to pause here
for a second with an analogy

00:50:14.580 --> 00:50:17.700
that to me makes a lot of sense,
and I hope it will make sense

00:50:17.700 --> 00:50:19.300
to you as well.

00:50:19.300 --> 00:50:27.300
So if we're in a room, where
there is absolutely no light,

00:50:27.300 --> 00:50:33.400
and if I just turn on
one light bulb, to you,

00:50:33.400 --> 00:50:36.100
the difference between
the complete darkness

00:50:36.100 --> 00:50:40.220
and one light bulb is a lot.

00:50:40.220 --> 00:50:45.580
But if in this room we have all
these light bulbs already on,

00:50:45.580 --> 00:50:50.060
and if I turn on yet
another light bulb,

00:50:50.060 --> 00:50:54.100
the perceptual difference
between having that extra light

00:50:54.100 --> 00:50:58.260
bulb in this setting
and the one light

00:50:58.260 --> 00:51:04.220
bulb that is turned on versus
darkness is very different.

00:51:04.220 --> 00:51:09.900
So this is what this
log tries to do, so if--

00:51:09.900 --> 00:51:12.140
you almost don't
have any errors.

00:51:12.140 --> 00:51:15.300
It's very, very small value.

00:51:15.300 --> 00:51:18.980
And you have just a
little bit more error.

00:51:18.980 --> 00:51:23.140
From your perspective,
it will be more something

00:51:23.140 --> 00:51:25.900
that you would want to notice
in terms of the variation,

00:51:25.900 --> 00:51:29.440
as opposed to if you have an
image that is quite wrong,

00:51:29.440 --> 00:51:35.460
that is yet again, a little bit
more wrong with the same delta.

00:51:35.460 --> 00:51:38.500
So this is what the
logarithm tries to do.

00:51:38.500 --> 00:51:41.780
Well, it's great,
but it is still

00:51:41.780 --> 00:51:45.140
subject to this
pixel position, so it

00:51:45.140 --> 00:51:49.780
is sensitive to any shifting.

00:51:49.780 --> 00:51:52.540
And this is the reason why
these are not the only metrics

00:51:52.540 --> 00:51:54.740
that people use.

00:51:54.740 --> 00:51:58.500
And in particular,
there is one metric

00:51:58.500 --> 00:52:03.700
that you may see in
papers that looks at how

00:52:03.700 --> 00:52:05.940
these images are structured.

00:52:05.940 --> 00:52:09.580
So let me tell you a little
bit about what I mean here.

00:52:09.580 --> 00:52:16.300
So here, let's imagine we take
the original input and then

00:52:16.300 --> 00:52:20.780
the generated output, which
is trying to be that input.

00:52:20.780 --> 00:52:24.780
So what we're trying to do
is to see is, let's say,

00:52:24.780 --> 00:52:30.500
we take a given patch that
is in the same location

00:52:30.500 --> 00:52:33.180
in both images.

00:52:33.180 --> 00:52:36.180
What we're trying to do
is whether the two have

00:52:36.180 --> 00:52:39.060
the same overall structure.

00:52:39.060 --> 00:52:44.380
And what I mean by that
is, do the colors have

00:52:44.380 --> 00:52:45.640
the same intensity?

00:52:45.640 --> 00:52:48.460
Does it have the
same brightness?

00:52:48.460 --> 00:52:52.240
The second one can be, does
it have the same contrast?

00:52:52.240 --> 00:52:56.140
And so here we can think of
the variance in pixel values

00:52:56.140 --> 00:52:59.460
within that patch.

00:52:59.460 --> 00:53:02.500
And the third one
is, do pixels vary

00:53:02.500 --> 00:53:08.140
in the same fashion in the first
patch and in the second patch?

00:53:08.140 --> 00:53:12.380
So these are the three
dimensions we care about.

00:53:12.380 --> 00:53:15.500
And for each of
them, the first one

00:53:15.500 --> 00:53:20.580
would be around computing the
mean of the pixels in the patch

00:53:20.580 --> 00:53:23.780
in order to quantify
the brightness,

00:53:23.780 --> 00:53:27.500
or the color intensity
of the patch.

00:53:27.500 --> 00:53:28.980
The second one,
the contrast would

00:53:28.980 --> 00:53:32.980
be around quantifying how
much these pixels vary,

00:53:32.980 --> 00:53:37.220
so you can very well take
the variance of those pixels.

00:53:37.220 --> 00:53:40.860
And then the third one is
regarding the structure,

00:53:40.860 --> 00:53:49.540
and here, you can very well take
the covariance between these two

00:53:49.540 --> 00:53:52.060
patches.

00:53:52.060 --> 00:53:58.100
And what you will get when you
compare the two is a similarity

00:53:58.100 --> 00:54:02.300
quantity that here in
the luminance case,

00:54:02.300 --> 00:54:05.100
takes in the mean
of the first patch

00:54:05.100 --> 00:54:07.620
and the mean of
the second patch,

00:54:07.620 --> 00:54:11.620
and tries to make a
number out of them.

00:54:11.620 --> 00:54:15.140
So is it the first time
you're seeing this formula?

00:54:15.140 --> 00:54:17.460
Yeah?

00:54:17.460 --> 00:54:22.900
I don't want it for this
to be looking scary.

00:54:22.900 --> 00:54:28.360
So if you're looking at
the formula, what you see

00:54:28.360 --> 00:54:34.180
is there are two quantities,
so let's note them A and B,

00:54:34.180 --> 00:54:35.660
in this case.

00:54:35.660 --> 00:54:44.300
And you have something 2AB
over A squared plus B squared.

00:54:44.300 --> 00:54:48.540
So you also have a constant that
is just for stability purposes,

00:54:48.540 --> 00:54:52.260
just in case both of them are 0.

00:54:52.260 --> 00:55:01.140
But in this case, you can assume
that A and B are greater than 0.

00:55:01.140 --> 00:55:04.940
So what can you say
about this quantity?

00:55:04.940 --> 00:55:11.660
So from high school, you may
remember that you have this very

00:55:11.660 --> 00:55:16.560
general identity, which is as
follows, so A minus B squared,

00:55:16.560 --> 00:55:21.620
which is a squared minus
2AB plus B squared.

00:55:21.620 --> 00:55:32.740
Well, here you can rewrite this
as A squared plus B squared,

00:55:32.740 --> 00:55:40.840
minus A minus B squared, over
A squared plus B squared.

00:55:43.620 --> 00:55:49.640
So I'm going to
replace in place.

00:55:49.640 --> 00:55:52.560
And so here what I'm going to
say is that this is equal to 1.

00:55:55.740 --> 00:56:03.980
I need to say here this is
equal to 1 minus this quantity.

00:56:03.980 --> 00:56:11.980
And here, this is positive,
so this is bounded by 1.

00:56:11.980 --> 00:56:14.200
And A is positive,
B is positive.

00:56:14.200 --> 00:56:16.380
This quantity is positive.

00:56:16.380 --> 00:56:19.340
This is bounded by 0.

00:56:19.340 --> 00:56:21.980
So this is a coefficient
that is between 0 and 1.

00:56:21.980 --> 00:56:24.620
This is what I'm trying to say.

00:56:24.620 --> 00:56:32.820
So here, if A and B are
similar, so if A is equal to B,

00:56:32.820 --> 00:56:34.980
this is equal to 0.

00:56:34.980 --> 00:56:37.060
It's equal to 1.

00:56:37.060 --> 00:56:41.100
So what I'm trying
to say is this metric

00:56:41.100 --> 00:56:48.500
is a similarity metric that
you may read on the papers.

00:56:48.500 --> 00:56:51.140
It's called the
Dice coefficient.

00:56:51.140 --> 00:56:54.340
And the reason why
it's a good metric

00:56:54.340 --> 00:56:59.620
is because it gives you the
similarity of two quantities

00:56:59.620 --> 00:57:04.540
by considering also the
values that they're at.

00:57:04.540 --> 00:57:09.420
So I'm going to illustrate
it with an example.

00:57:09.420 --> 00:57:15.500
So this quantity, so if
let's say, A is equal to 10

00:57:15.500 --> 00:57:19.900
and B is equal to 20, then
let's say that quantity

00:57:19.900 --> 00:57:25.560
that I'm going to let's say d.

00:57:25.560 --> 00:57:27.560
d for Dice coefficient.

00:57:27.560 --> 00:57:34.820
So that d will be equal
to 0.8, if you compute it.

00:57:34.820 --> 00:57:39.500
But if A is equal to
100 and B is equal to,

00:57:39.500 --> 00:57:46.580
let's say, 110, then d is
going to be much closer to 1.

00:57:46.580 --> 00:57:50.900
And B maybe something
somewhere around 0.995.

00:57:50.900 --> 00:57:53.620
So I computed this before
the lecture, so don't worry.

00:57:53.620 --> 00:57:57.740
I'm not hallucinating things.

00:57:57.740 --> 00:58:03.300
But here, what I want
to say is that this idea

00:58:03.300 --> 00:58:08.260
of considering the
level that you're at

00:58:08.260 --> 00:58:11.780
and considering the relative
difference with respect

00:58:11.780 --> 00:58:15.260
to where you're at, which
we saw with the logarithm

00:58:15.260 --> 00:58:18.460
and with the light bulb
story, is also something

00:58:18.460 --> 00:58:20.460
that is captured here.

00:58:20.460 --> 00:58:28.860
So even though A and
B are 10 apart, here,

00:58:28.860 --> 00:58:31.480
they are less similar than here.

00:58:34.220 --> 00:58:38.820
So all of that to say
that I hope that when

00:58:38.820 --> 00:58:42.500
you look at this
formula, it doesn't

00:58:42.500 --> 00:58:46.420
look as something that
came out of the blue.

00:58:46.420 --> 00:58:50.660
So in this formula
on the slide, we're

00:58:50.660 --> 00:58:54.660
using this way of
computing similarity using

00:58:54.660 --> 00:58:57.700
the mean of the
pixels, which gives you

00:58:57.700 --> 00:59:00.540
the luminance similarity.

00:59:00.540 --> 00:59:02.280
You can do the
same for contrast,

00:59:02.280 --> 00:59:06.820
so here you use the variance
and the standard deviation.

00:59:06.820 --> 00:59:08.720
And you also have one
for the structure.

00:59:08.720 --> 00:59:11.660
Well, this one is a
little bit different.

00:59:11.660 --> 00:59:16.160
You're actually using
the Pearson correlation,

00:59:16.160 --> 00:59:21.220
which gives you a quantity
that is between minus 1 and 1.

00:59:21.220 --> 00:59:23.440
So let's assume you've
done that for luminance,

00:59:23.440 --> 00:59:25.980
for contrast, for structure.

00:59:25.980 --> 00:59:29.180
And just for simplicity,
let's note them L, C,

00:59:29.180 --> 00:59:36.380
and S. What we're doing is we're
just multiplying everything.

00:59:36.380 --> 00:59:39.940
So if things are similar,
they're equal to 1.

00:59:39.940 --> 00:59:44.840
If things are not similar,
then L is more equal to 0,

00:59:44.840 --> 00:59:48.060
C is tending to 0, and
S, the correlation,

00:59:48.060 --> 00:59:49.760
is tending to minus 1.

00:59:53.660 --> 00:59:56.300
And so this is exactly
what we mentioned.

00:59:56.300 --> 01:00:00.460
And as a result, you are
getting this formula, which

01:00:00.460 --> 01:00:02.980
you may have seen in textbooks.

01:00:02.980 --> 01:00:06.380
It looks really scary when
you just look at this formula

01:00:06.380 --> 01:00:10.880
firsthand, but with this
way of looking at things,

01:00:10.880 --> 01:00:15.300
I hope it will not
look scary anymore.

01:00:15.300 --> 01:00:19.860
And so if you do that
operation for all patches,

01:00:19.860 --> 01:00:22.620
and the resulting
metric that you obtain

01:00:22.620 --> 01:00:28.300
is the average of those
scores across your patches.

01:00:28.300 --> 01:00:31.540
And you get a metric that
is called the SSIM, so

01:00:31.540 --> 01:00:37.260
the Structural SIMilarity,
which is a score between minus 1

01:00:37.260 --> 01:00:44.100
and 1, where the closer you
are to 1, the more similar

01:00:44.100 --> 01:00:47.320
the images should be from
a structural perspective.

01:00:51.620 --> 01:00:53.840
But you may tell me,
well, it's on patches.

01:00:53.840 --> 01:00:56.560
What if your shift
is beyond the patch?

01:00:56.560 --> 01:00:58.940
And I would say, yeah,
you're completely right.

01:00:58.940 --> 01:01:02.860
This metric, although
being less pixel-dependent

01:01:02.860 --> 01:01:06.220
than the MSC and
the PSNR is still

01:01:06.220 --> 01:01:09.020
sensitive to that
pixel shift, which

01:01:09.020 --> 01:01:12.900
is the reason why there
is a final metric I want

01:01:12.900 --> 01:01:16.380
to talk to you about, which
we actually have already

01:01:16.380 --> 01:01:23.180
seen in lecture, I believe,
4, which is the LPIPS,

01:01:23.180 --> 01:01:27.460
the Learned Perceptual
Image Patch Similarity.

01:01:27.460 --> 01:01:31.340
And that one is
actually quite simple.

01:01:31.340 --> 01:01:37.220
Instead of comparing your x with
your x hat in the pixel space,

01:01:37.220 --> 01:01:42.540
what you do is you
take each quantity,

01:01:42.540 --> 01:01:46.540
and you let them through
a pre-trained encoder.

01:01:46.540 --> 01:01:49.260
And you compute
the representation

01:01:49.260 --> 01:01:52.300
that this encoder
has, which tells

01:01:52.300 --> 01:01:56.880
you more about how
perceptually similar they are,

01:01:56.880 --> 01:01:59.380
as opposed to exactly
how aligned they

01:01:59.380 --> 01:02:03.260
are from a pixel perspective.

01:02:03.260 --> 01:02:05.980
And so here, you
have this distance

01:02:05.980 --> 01:02:09.520
between phi Lx and phi Lx hat.

01:02:09.520 --> 01:02:15.060
So the L represents the layer
of that pre-trained encoder.

01:02:15.060 --> 01:02:18.980
And you also have
a coefficient w,

01:02:18.980 --> 01:02:21.500
which is actually
a coefficient that

01:02:21.500 --> 01:02:27.300
was determined such that
the resulting metric aligns

01:02:27.300 --> 01:02:30.860
with human perception.

01:02:30.860 --> 01:02:33.460
So I believe, these
were tunes because there

01:02:33.460 --> 01:02:36.480
was a data set of images.

01:02:36.480 --> 01:02:40.300
And the idea was
to tune this metric

01:02:40.300 --> 01:02:42.620
so that it's as aligned
as possible with

01:02:42.620 --> 01:02:46.900
the human perception levels.

01:02:46.900 --> 01:02:51.140
And that metric is actually
quite commonly used,

01:02:51.140 --> 01:02:53.540
and so you may see
it quite often.

01:02:53.540 --> 01:02:57.100
The only problem to call
out, only top of mind,

01:02:57.100 --> 01:03:00.740
is that it's not super
interpretable in terms

01:03:00.740 --> 01:03:03.180
of what is going wrong.

01:03:03.180 --> 01:03:08.400
So if your metric
is not doing great,

01:03:08.400 --> 01:03:10.200
you need to see what's going on.

01:03:12.900 --> 01:03:15.140
And that was it.

01:03:15.140 --> 01:03:17.940
Any questions before-- yep.

01:03:17.940 --> 01:03:21.700
So the question is for this last
one, which pre-trained model is

01:03:21.700 --> 01:03:22.700
there to use?

01:03:22.700 --> 01:03:26.400
So I believe it is VGG.

01:03:26.400 --> 01:03:29.620
So there's AlexNet as well.

01:03:29.620 --> 01:03:33.380
The reason why we have
some preset encoders

01:03:33.380 --> 01:03:37.980
is because the coefficients Wl
are dependent on the encoder

01:03:37.980 --> 01:03:40.820
that you use.

01:03:40.820 --> 01:03:44.740
So you can use the
libraries and mention

01:03:44.740 --> 01:03:47.680
which encoder you
want to leverage,

01:03:47.680 --> 01:03:50.900
but typically, it's
either VGG or AlexNet.

01:03:50.900 --> 01:03:53.380
So the question is, what
does the formula mean?

01:03:53.380 --> 01:03:58.580
So within the distance,
what you have is two terms.

01:03:58.580 --> 01:04:02.420
One is the difference
between the feature map

01:04:02.420 --> 01:04:06.580
of x and feature map of x hat.

01:04:06.580 --> 01:04:10.060
And you multiply
that elementwise

01:04:10.060 --> 01:04:11.540
with the coefficient Wl.

01:04:15.490 --> 01:04:17.450
Does that make sense?

01:04:17.450 --> 01:04:20.490
Perfect.

01:04:20.490 --> 01:04:22.910
So the question is this
was for two images.

01:04:22.910 --> 01:04:24.450
How do you apply
it to the batch?

01:04:24.450 --> 01:04:28.010
So just as a reminder,
we're looking

01:04:28.010 --> 01:04:33.770
at cases where we care
about how good of a job

01:04:33.770 --> 01:04:37.850
we did about
reconstructing an input.

01:04:37.850 --> 01:04:42.450
So what you care about
is how far off you are.

01:04:42.450 --> 01:04:44.930
And so this metric tells
you how far off you

01:04:44.930 --> 01:04:48.990
are in trying to
reconstruct an input,

01:04:48.990 --> 01:04:50.490
and so if you want
to aggregate, you

01:04:50.490 --> 01:04:52.770
can use the traditional
aggregations

01:04:52.770 --> 01:04:55.270
like mean and so on.

01:04:58.370 --> 01:05:00.370
Cool.

01:05:00.370 --> 01:05:01.570
Great.

01:05:01.570 --> 01:05:04.250
With that, I'll
give it to Shervin.

01:05:04.250 --> 01:05:06.050
Thank you, Afshin.

01:05:06.050 --> 01:05:10.150
So exactly as we saw,
all of these metrics,

01:05:10.150 --> 01:05:13.610
they're very mathematical,
and what we want

01:05:13.610 --> 01:05:18.730
is have a way to interpret them
from an intuition standpoint.

01:05:18.730 --> 01:05:23.730
So we saw that all
these metrics, they

01:05:23.730 --> 01:05:26.170
were operating at
different levels

01:05:26.170 --> 01:05:27.810
and there are many of them.

01:05:27.810 --> 01:05:32.410
So what I'm going to do is
invite you to do a little detour

01:05:32.410 --> 01:05:37.170
and visit a class of models
that we call multi-modal LLMs,

01:05:37.170 --> 01:05:39.530
because they have
the nice property

01:05:39.530 --> 01:05:45.610
of being able to convert text
and image data in a text.

01:05:45.610 --> 01:05:49.690
But before we do that,
let's do a little summary

01:05:49.690 --> 01:05:52.610
of the kinds of models we've
seen so far in the class.

01:05:52.610 --> 01:05:55.690
So we saw the
transformer at some point

01:05:55.690 --> 01:05:58.570
that we know
transforms bits of text

01:05:58.570 --> 01:06:00.210
to text, so this
is not something

01:06:00.210 --> 01:06:02.530
that we can use out of the box.

01:06:02.530 --> 01:06:08.090
We saw DIT, where based on
latent noise and potentially

01:06:08.090 --> 01:06:10.930
a condition, you could
generate an image.

01:06:10.930 --> 01:06:13.450
So here, it's a
generation of images.

01:06:13.450 --> 01:06:17.770
This is also a model that
we cannot reuse as is.

01:06:17.770 --> 01:06:22.250
And same for MMDIT,
where the input was

01:06:22.250 --> 01:06:26.650
guided by potentially
texts, not just a condition.

01:06:26.650 --> 01:06:30.130
And here, we're in
this sibling problem

01:06:30.130 --> 01:06:32.610
of dealing with both
modalities, except that we

01:06:32.610 --> 01:06:36.850
want image and text
at the input in order

01:06:36.850 --> 01:06:43.370
to find a way to judge them
and get text as output.

01:06:43.370 --> 01:06:48.190
So this is typically the setup
that we're interested in,

01:06:48.190 --> 01:06:49.650
so we have a model.

01:06:49.650 --> 01:06:53.590
We have a picture, let's
say, here a teddy bear,

01:06:53.590 --> 01:06:57.130
and we ask a question that
would guide the evaluation.

01:06:57.130 --> 01:06:59.570
So let's say, how cute
is this teddy bear?

01:06:59.570 --> 01:07:01.930
And you would want such
an evaluation model

01:07:01.930 --> 01:07:04.610
to give you the answer.

01:07:04.610 --> 01:07:07.810
So before starting
all of this, I just

01:07:07.810 --> 01:07:11.270
want to make the problem
a bit more concrete.

01:07:11.270 --> 01:07:12.550
What do we have at hand?

01:07:12.550 --> 01:07:14.490
So when we talk
about images and when

01:07:14.490 --> 01:07:19.730
we talk about text, what we can
do is convert them to tokens.

01:07:19.730 --> 01:07:22.570
And at the end of
the day, tokens,

01:07:22.570 --> 01:07:26.130
they're just
embeddings, so we're

01:07:26.130 --> 01:07:30.130
facing the problem of trying
to find an architecture that

01:07:30.130 --> 01:07:32.330
can accept these
embeddings and turn them

01:07:32.330 --> 01:07:35.490
into interpretable text.

01:07:35.490 --> 01:07:40.090
So one thing that comes
to mind-- so if you recall

01:07:40.090 --> 01:07:42.690
the architecture
of the transformer,

01:07:42.690 --> 01:07:46.970
let's say you have
a text-based model.

01:07:46.970 --> 01:07:51.290
One natural choice
could be to leverage

01:07:51.290 --> 01:07:54.970
that cross-attention
layer that could

01:07:54.970 --> 01:08:00.490
enable you to make text tokens,
attend to anything you want.

01:08:00.490 --> 01:08:03.610
And that anything you
want, the keys and values

01:08:03.610 --> 01:08:08.290
of cross-attention could
very well be images.

01:08:08.290 --> 01:08:10.730
So this is one
possible direction,

01:08:10.730 --> 01:08:17.450
where you use text tokens
as input of your decoder,

01:08:17.450 --> 01:08:22.050
and then you keep this cross
attention mechanism in order

01:08:22.050 --> 01:08:25.890
to make it interact
with encoded images.

01:08:25.890 --> 01:08:29.490
So this is something
that people have

01:08:29.490 --> 01:08:31.950
done because it's a very
natural thing to do,

01:08:31.950 --> 01:08:36.050
and it's also quite efficient
computationally because you

01:08:36.050 --> 01:08:40.670
leverage a fixed context
length only composed of texts,

01:08:40.670 --> 01:08:43.890
and then you make it
interact with images.

01:08:43.890 --> 01:08:46.490
So there is one example
here that we can

01:08:46.490 --> 01:08:49.649
cite of a model that did so.

01:08:49.649 --> 01:08:53.450
So Flamingo by
Google, where images

01:08:53.450 --> 01:08:58.490
are given as keys and values,
and you have some placeholder

01:08:58.490 --> 01:09:05.250
token that gates when it's
fine to attend to these images.

01:09:05.250 --> 01:09:07.569
So this is one possibility.

01:09:07.569 --> 01:09:14.210
But one caveat of this mindset
is that you cannot directly

01:09:14.210 --> 01:09:18.250
leverage the latest advances
in large language models

01:09:18.250 --> 01:09:23.149
because as you know, these
are decoder only models,

01:09:23.149 --> 01:09:28.750
and they have gotten rid of
that cross-attention altogether,

01:09:28.750 --> 01:09:31.490
so you cannot use all these
latest models that have been

01:09:31.490 --> 01:09:35.529
tuned and work great
out of the box.

01:09:35.529 --> 01:09:40.850
You need to re-engineer that
cross-attention layer in there.

01:09:40.850 --> 01:09:45.609
So this is why you
see a second branch

01:09:45.609 --> 01:09:48.770
of dealing with
multi-modal input, which

01:09:48.770 --> 01:09:54.810
is to directly feed this image
and text tokens as input.

01:09:54.810 --> 01:10:01.110
And one example of
such model is a LLaVA.

01:10:01.110 --> 01:10:07.930
So here, you use an encoder
to find the right embedding

01:10:07.930 --> 01:10:12.610
for those tokens for image
patches like a VAT model,

01:10:12.610 --> 01:10:17.190
and then you give it to
that decoder-only structure.

01:10:19.970 --> 01:10:20.610
Great.

01:10:20.610 --> 01:10:23.130
And these days,
exactly for the reason

01:10:23.130 --> 01:10:27.290
that I mentioned like
quality, but also

01:10:27.290 --> 01:10:31.050
the fact of reusing wins,
this latter technique

01:10:31.050 --> 01:10:32.930
is what people use in practice.

01:10:32.930 --> 01:10:36.730
So all the latest MLLM
that you might see

01:10:36.730 --> 01:10:41.930
are based oftentimes
on putting both images

01:10:41.930 --> 01:10:43.950
and text as input
of the decoder,

01:10:43.950 --> 01:10:47.450
not doing cross attention.

01:10:47.450 --> 01:10:52.750
And they have some capabilities
that I'm mentioning here.

01:10:52.750 --> 01:10:56.410
We're not going to go
into details about them,

01:10:56.410 --> 01:10:59.090
but I want you to
be aware of them

01:10:59.090 --> 01:11:02.190
because we're going to
actively use them at the time

01:11:02.190 --> 01:11:05.970
we want to use these
models for judging.

01:11:05.970 --> 01:11:11.490
So one is a working with
all kinds of images.

01:11:11.490 --> 01:11:14.050
So there's a question here.

01:11:14.050 --> 01:11:17.890
So the question is, what do you
mean by reuse existing wins?

01:11:17.890 --> 01:11:22.490
So it's an engineering question.

01:11:22.490 --> 01:11:26.290
You could definitely reuse
something already trained

01:11:26.290 --> 01:11:29.150
like have weights frozen
and start from there.

01:11:29.150 --> 01:11:31.210
This is one possibility.

01:11:31.210 --> 01:11:35.930
I was more thinking about an
architectural standpoint, where

01:11:35.930 --> 01:11:39.610
you have a design that
is proven to work.

01:11:39.610 --> 01:11:43.450
This is just another
modality with a same input

01:11:43.450 --> 01:11:45.770
that you can just
recycle, but then

01:11:45.770 --> 01:11:48.370
on how you actually come
up with such a model,

01:11:48.370 --> 01:11:51.330
it's more of an
engineering problem.

01:11:51.330 --> 01:11:54.070
So I don't give a
specific opinion on it,

01:11:54.070 --> 01:11:56.410
but I do think you
could definitely

01:11:56.410 --> 01:12:02.350
initialize weights and reuse it
in the strict sense of the term.

01:12:05.390 --> 01:12:09.730
So we want these
models to be working

01:12:09.730 --> 01:12:11.850
across all kinds of inputs.

01:12:11.850 --> 01:12:14.570
And in particular,
one property that you

01:12:14.570 --> 01:12:18.930
want your generative
models is to generate text

01:12:18.930 --> 01:12:20.150
as part of your images.

01:12:20.150 --> 01:12:24.570
So let's say if you want to
generate the image of a sign,

01:12:24.570 --> 01:12:27.570
you want the text
to be readable,

01:12:27.570 --> 01:12:30.370
so you would want
models that do judge

01:12:30.370 --> 01:12:35.750
this kind of generated images
to be aware of characters.

01:12:35.750 --> 01:12:39.710
So this is why I mentioned OCR.

01:12:39.710 --> 01:12:43.930
And then also one other thing
which I think was mentioned here

01:12:43.930 --> 01:12:47.970
is we do not want to have a
black box that just gives us

01:12:47.970 --> 01:12:48.930
a number.

01:12:48.930 --> 01:12:54.750
We want to have some rationale
behind what grades that we give,

01:12:54.750 --> 01:12:59.010
so this is why leveraging
reasoning abilities

01:12:59.010 --> 01:13:01.910
of such models is going to be
something that we want to use.

01:13:05.070 --> 01:13:07.250
Great.

01:13:07.250 --> 01:13:12.090
So now, I want us to look
at traditional metrics

01:13:12.090 --> 01:13:16.130
and then find some of
the key concerns we had

01:13:16.130 --> 01:13:19.810
and see what could be
ways to mitigate them.

01:13:19.810 --> 01:13:24.010
So one thing that
we were concerned

01:13:24.010 --> 01:13:29.090
with with traditional metrics
was that you get a number,

01:13:29.090 --> 01:13:33.550
but at the end of the day,
you don't what to do with it.

01:13:33.550 --> 01:13:37.570
Let's say you have a prompt
that gave a given image

01:13:37.570 --> 01:13:39.610
and you have a
certain CLIP score.

01:13:39.610 --> 01:13:41.930
Now what do you do with it?

01:13:41.930 --> 01:13:48.970
So this is something that you
could fix by taking the prompt

01:13:48.970 --> 01:13:54.370
and trying to reason
about what properties

01:13:54.370 --> 01:13:57.730
in the generated image
could make it such

01:13:57.730 --> 01:14:02.450
that the image would
match the prompt?

01:14:02.450 --> 01:14:05.570
And this is something
that you could

01:14:05.570 --> 01:14:10.850
do by decomposing what you care
about into atomic properties.

01:14:10.850 --> 01:14:14.350
This is what the
TIFA paper does,

01:14:14.350 --> 01:14:17.970
so Text-to-Image
Faithfulness Evaluation,

01:14:17.970 --> 01:14:23.810
where they use few shot learning
to show examples of what

01:14:23.810 --> 01:14:27.010
interesting decompositions of
what you care about in an image

01:14:27.010 --> 01:14:28.370
might look like.

01:14:28.370 --> 01:14:30.690
And they apply it
to a given prompt

01:14:30.690 --> 01:14:34.530
to generate a set of
questions, and these questions

01:14:34.530 --> 01:14:35.750
are very simple.

01:14:35.750 --> 01:14:40.690
They're just yes or no, or
things where the answer is

01:14:40.690 --> 01:14:43.330
obviously right or wrong.

01:14:43.330 --> 01:14:48.970
So that way you
decompose the judging

01:14:48.970 --> 01:14:54.890
into dimensions that
are quite quantitative.

01:14:54.890 --> 01:14:59.210
And what you do is that
you take MLLM model

01:14:59.210 --> 01:15:02.970
to assess each of these
dimensions independently.

01:15:02.970 --> 01:15:06.490
So for example here, this
teddy bear reading a book.

01:15:06.490 --> 01:15:07.590
Is it a teddy bear?

01:15:07.590 --> 01:15:08.670
So yes.

01:15:08.670 --> 01:15:09.330
Is it cute?

01:15:09.330 --> 01:15:09.850
Yes.

01:15:09.850 --> 01:15:10.930
Is there a book?

01:15:10.930 --> 01:15:11.490
Yes.

01:15:11.490 --> 01:15:13.115
And is the Teddy bear
reading the book?

01:15:13.115 --> 01:15:14.250
Yes as well.

01:15:14.250 --> 01:15:17.690
So such a metric
would give you a score

01:15:17.690 --> 01:15:23.810
that matches the fact that all
of these criteria were met.

01:15:23.810 --> 01:15:29.570
And it's quite
interesting because

01:15:29.570 --> 01:15:31.770
in the case of CLIP
score, even though you

01:15:31.770 --> 01:15:36.530
have an image that semantically
matches your input prompt,

01:15:36.530 --> 01:15:39.870
you're not exactly sure what
that remaining delta is about.

01:15:39.870 --> 01:15:42.750
And with the method that
decomposes these concerns,

01:15:42.750 --> 01:15:44.910
you could pinpoint
what goes wrong.

01:15:48.070 --> 01:15:53.090
So it's quite simple,
and you could debug

01:15:53.090 --> 01:15:54.470
where the issue comes from.

01:15:54.470 --> 01:15:57.130
But on the other
hand, this method

01:15:57.130 --> 01:16:00.890
of generating dimensions
that you care about per image

01:16:00.890 --> 01:16:03.770
is quite bespoke
because you're here.

01:16:03.770 --> 01:16:08.650
At every prompt that
you input your data set,

01:16:08.650 --> 01:16:14.450
you need to have a
grading rubric that

01:16:14.450 --> 01:16:16.250
is specific to that prompt.

01:16:16.250 --> 01:16:19.170
So the fact of generating
that rubric in the first place

01:16:19.170 --> 01:16:23.410
is going to be expensive,
and it might be error prone.

01:16:23.410 --> 01:16:27.970
And something else is that you
have all of these questions that

01:16:27.970 --> 01:16:31.610
examine multiple
sides of that prompt,

01:16:31.610 --> 01:16:35.850
but the weighing of
each of these claims

01:16:35.850 --> 01:16:40.130
might not match the
importance of what each

01:16:40.130 --> 01:16:42.710
claim to your point of view.

01:16:42.710 --> 01:16:48.450
So, for example, here, if
you don't have a book at all,

01:16:48.450 --> 01:16:52.050
then you might have one
or two that goes away.

01:16:52.050 --> 01:16:57.690
And you would have maybe
half of the metric that--

01:16:57.690 --> 01:17:00.110
the metric would be about
half of the max score,

01:17:00.110 --> 01:17:03.930
but it necessarily might
not be what you want,

01:17:03.930 --> 01:17:10.330
so you might want a way
to generate a rubric that

01:17:10.330 --> 01:17:13.790
is more robust to that.

01:17:18.010 --> 01:17:20.410
I'm going to take a
pause for a few seconds

01:17:20.410 --> 01:17:21.990
and see if that makes sense.

01:17:27.610 --> 01:17:29.090
OK, awesome.

01:17:29.090 --> 01:17:31.870
So I'm going to state
another complaint.

01:17:34.430 --> 01:17:37.730
So here we saw CLIP score.

01:17:37.730 --> 01:17:40.610
And one great thing
about CLIP score

01:17:40.610 --> 01:17:45.210
is that it can try to convey
whether the semantic meaning

01:17:45.210 --> 01:17:48.010
of a generated
image was preserved.

01:17:48.010 --> 01:17:53.810
But one thing that we observe
is that semantically encoding

01:17:53.810 --> 01:17:56.510
the meaning of a
prompt within a vector,

01:17:56.510 --> 01:17:59.290
and then doing some dot products
with respect to the embedding

01:17:59.290 --> 01:18:04.410
of the image, might not carry
all the semantic subtleties

01:18:04.410 --> 01:18:07.010
that prompt variations
might convey.

01:18:07.010 --> 01:18:10.330
So here, one thing that
we observe in practice

01:18:10.330 --> 01:18:16.290
is that in the cute bear reading
a book example, if you exchange

01:18:16.290 --> 01:18:22.050
book and teddy bear from
a semantic perspective

01:18:22.050 --> 01:18:25.290
like to your intuition, it
changes things quite a bit.

01:18:25.290 --> 01:18:29.250
You would have a totally
another image in mind.

01:18:29.250 --> 01:18:31.970
But from a CLIP
score standpoint,

01:18:31.970 --> 01:18:35.690
it turns out that the score
doesn't change that much.

01:18:35.690 --> 01:18:39.460
So you could explain
this by the fact

01:18:39.460 --> 01:18:43.170
that you're projecting
all of the image

01:18:43.170 --> 01:18:46.470
and all of the content of
the prompt into vectors,

01:18:46.470 --> 01:18:53.130
and these are not very richly
conveying all these subtleties.

01:18:53.130 --> 01:18:57.250
But also one other explanation
could reside in the way

01:18:57.250 --> 01:19:03.490
that CLIP is trained,
because you are incentivized

01:19:03.490 --> 01:19:09.130
to match images and prompts in
a way that matches those that

01:19:09.130 --> 01:19:12.490
correspond with respect
to text image captions

01:19:12.490 --> 01:19:15.010
but you have this concept
of in batch negatives

01:19:15.010 --> 01:19:19.210
that we saw at the time, where
we introduced CLIP, where you

01:19:19.210 --> 01:19:22.490
try to make the
distance between images

01:19:22.490 --> 01:19:26.090
and prompts that have
nothing to do with that image

01:19:26.090 --> 01:19:30.490
further apart, so
you don't really

01:19:30.490 --> 01:19:33.410
have an incentive to
learn these subtleties.

01:19:33.410 --> 01:19:39.050
And these are the two main
reasons why CLIP score is not

01:19:39.050 --> 01:19:42.110
that great.

01:19:42.110 --> 01:19:43.430
But you might tell me OK.

01:19:43.430 --> 01:19:47.530
Hey, Shervin, CLIP was
generated with the embedding

01:19:47.530 --> 01:19:52.290
of the entire sentence
because, as we remember,

01:19:52.290 --> 01:19:58.550
you would encode the meaning of
a given sentence with a decoder,

01:19:58.550 --> 01:20:01.210
and then extract the
last embedding, which

01:20:01.210 --> 01:20:03.730
contains the embedding
of the whole sentence.

01:20:03.730 --> 01:20:12.570
So one thing we can do
is have the same property

01:20:12.570 --> 01:20:17.490
with the trick, where you input
both the image and the sentence

01:20:17.490 --> 01:20:21.570
in the same embedding, and
then extract the probability

01:20:21.570 --> 01:20:27.370
of whether both image and
sentence are supposed to match

01:20:27.370 --> 01:20:27.870
or not.

01:20:27.870 --> 01:20:31.650
So this is something that
is called VQA score, where

01:20:31.650 --> 01:20:33.870
the template is very simple.

01:20:33.870 --> 01:20:37.250
So you always follow
this same structure

01:20:37.250 --> 01:20:38.930
where you put the image.

01:20:38.930 --> 01:20:41.770
And then you ask
yourself, does this figure

01:20:41.770 --> 01:20:44.530
show and the content
of your prompt?

01:20:44.530 --> 01:20:47.690
And then what you do is that
you look at your probability

01:20:47.690 --> 01:20:52.050
distribution of your
next token, and you

01:20:52.050 --> 01:20:56.210
look at the one that
corresponds to the token yes.

01:20:56.210 --> 01:20:59.370
And you say that
that probability

01:20:59.370 --> 01:21:05.210
is going to be your score, which
means that if the sentence is

01:21:05.210 --> 01:21:10.030
semantically similar or
matching to the image,

01:21:10.030 --> 01:21:15.290
you're going to have a high
probability and lower otherwise.

01:21:15.290 --> 01:21:21.290
So by doing so, you can
alleviate this issue

01:21:21.290 --> 01:21:24.250
that we had seen at the CLIP
score level, where you were

01:21:24.250 --> 01:21:27.310
comparing vectors
in a coarse fashion,

01:21:27.310 --> 01:21:30.290
and now you're directly
reusing the structure

01:21:30.290 --> 01:21:35.450
of the decoded output in
order to embed both modalities

01:21:35.450 --> 01:21:36.950
in a way that makes sense.

01:21:41.250 --> 01:21:43.730
So the question, can
a regular LLM do it?

01:21:43.730 --> 01:21:46.010
So very pedantically,
no, because you

01:21:46.010 --> 01:21:47.710
have image modality in there.

01:21:47.710 --> 01:21:49.970
But let's say the
question is for MLLM.

01:21:49.970 --> 01:21:52.470
Can a standard MLLM do it?

01:21:52.470 --> 01:21:59.010
So we advocate that yes,
because it is trained yet

01:21:59.010 --> 01:22:06.090
to understand broader concepts,
so its a zero shot task.

01:22:06.090 --> 01:22:06.590
So yeah.

01:22:06.590 --> 01:22:07.630
So that's a great point.

01:22:07.630 --> 01:22:09.710
It emphasizes one benefit
of this technique.

01:22:09.710 --> 01:22:12.870
You do not need to train
anything in a bespoke manner.

01:22:12.870 --> 01:22:17.130
You just take an
off the shelf MLLM

01:22:17.130 --> 01:22:21.430
that does know how
to understand things.

01:22:25.050 --> 01:22:28.370
OK great.

01:22:28.370 --> 01:22:32.090
But one drawback
that you might notice

01:22:32.090 --> 01:22:36.130
is that this technique
relies on you having access

01:22:36.130 --> 01:22:40.370
to the probability
distribution of the next token.

01:22:40.370 --> 01:22:44.170
And if you take all the
latest and best models

01:22:44.170 --> 01:22:46.490
out there that might
be closed source,

01:22:46.490 --> 01:22:49.890
they do not disclose the
probability distribution

01:22:49.890 --> 01:22:50.669
of the next token.

01:22:50.669 --> 01:22:52.336
If you are in the
field of distillation,

01:22:52.336 --> 01:22:54.410
you know it's a
very sensitive topic

01:22:54.410 --> 01:22:56.930
because you can learn a
lot of things from it,

01:22:56.930 --> 01:22:59.850
so this is not something
that you have access to.

01:22:59.850 --> 01:23:03.210
And something else
that is wasteful

01:23:03.210 --> 01:23:06.510
is every time you
ask such questions,

01:23:06.510 --> 01:23:10.370
you need to do a
dedicated MLLM call,

01:23:10.370 --> 01:23:12.510
and these are quite expensive.

01:23:12.510 --> 01:23:15.510
So from a user standpoint,
you could say, OK fine,

01:23:15.510 --> 01:23:18.970
I have a provider, I will
do all of them in parallel

01:23:18.970 --> 01:23:21.342
and wait for the
max of all of them

01:23:21.342 --> 01:23:23.050
to finish to have my
result, which is not

01:23:23.050 --> 01:23:25.890
that bad oftentimes,
but it's quite costly

01:23:25.890 --> 01:23:31.130
as it scales with the number
of decomposed dimensions.

01:23:31.130 --> 01:23:34.250
And everything that
I've mentioned here

01:23:34.250 --> 01:23:38.750
is focusing on the
prompts of your data set.

01:23:38.750 --> 01:23:41.850
So you're inferring your
conditions and dimensions

01:23:41.850 --> 01:23:44.570
above the prompt
of the data set.

01:23:44.570 --> 01:23:47.230
There seems like a
better way to do it.

01:23:47.230 --> 01:23:51.330
You're still stuck in
these ancient worlds,

01:23:51.330 --> 01:23:54.310
where you try to engineer
everything, even metrics.

01:23:54.310 --> 01:23:58.250
You try to engineer them in
order to make sense of them

01:23:58.250 --> 01:23:59.570
afterwards.

01:23:59.570 --> 01:24:05.530
But one shift of paradigm
that seems attractive here

01:24:05.530 --> 01:24:09.090
is instead of being
prompt-centric,

01:24:09.090 --> 01:24:14.890
be concept-centric of what
you consider to be good,

01:24:14.890 --> 01:24:19.090
and then describe that
good in a generic way.

01:24:19.090 --> 01:24:22.890
And then leverage all of
the reasoning capabilities

01:24:22.890 --> 01:24:28.050
of these MLLM models to do
that reasoning on your behalf

01:24:28.050 --> 01:24:31.170
and come up with
the final score.

01:24:31.170 --> 01:24:35.910
So does everyone agree that
this strategy sounds attractive?

01:24:39.490 --> 01:24:41.610
Great.

01:24:41.610 --> 01:24:46.770
And this brings
us to a paper that

01:24:46.770 --> 01:24:49.530
popularized this
mindset called VIEScore,

01:24:49.530 --> 01:24:54.730
so Visual Instruction-guided
Explainable Score, where

01:24:54.730 --> 01:24:58.810
you give your prompt, you
give your generated image,

01:24:58.810 --> 01:25:02.170
and then you describe a rubric.

01:25:02.170 --> 01:25:05.990
And Afshin, if you remember at
the beginning of the lecture,

01:25:05.990 --> 01:25:11.890
he mentioned two axes, where
we broadly had an interest in.

01:25:11.890 --> 01:25:14.530
So one is semantic
consistency, and the other one

01:25:14.530 --> 01:25:16.650
is perceptual quality.

01:25:16.650 --> 01:25:22.770
And these efforts formalizes
how we could write rubrics

01:25:22.770 --> 01:25:24.850
and grades for both of them.

01:25:24.850 --> 01:25:29.630
So you would have such a rubric,
and then ask an MLLM about it.

01:25:29.630 --> 01:25:32.450
And then since this
task is about judging,

01:25:32.450 --> 01:25:36.130
you call such a model
MLLM-as-a-Judge.

01:25:36.130 --> 01:25:39.650
And the good thing
about these models

01:25:39.650 --> 01:25:41.770
is that they are
interpretable, and you

01:25:41.770 --> 01:25:47.690
can ask them to output some
reasoning before prompting them

01:25:47.690 --> 01:25:52.090
for a given score, which
is what this paper does.

01:25:52.090 --> 01:25:58.090
And with our two dimensions
that we care about, so

01:25:58.090 --> 01:26:00.850
semantic consistency
and perceptual quality,

01:26:00.850 --> 01:26:07.090
respectively denoting whether
the image follows the prompt

01:26:07.090 --> 01:26:09.410
and whether the
image looks good.

01:26:09.410 --> 01:26:12.850
So you could have two different
generations and you might smile

01:26:12.850 --> 01:26:16.990
at the second one because
it's not very realistic,

01:26:16.990 --> 01:26:20.970
which is why you might-- so
it's technically still reading

01:26:20.970 --> 01:26:26.610
the book on the right-hand side,
but on the natural aspects,

01:26:26.610 --> 01:26:32.210
this is where an MLLM-as-a-Judge
might come in and just keep us

01:26:32.210 --> 01:26:32.870
in check.

01:26:36.250 --> 01:26:36.750
Great.

01:26:36.750 --> 01:26:41.930
And just formalizing the fact of
asking about an MLLM-as-a-Judge

01:26:41.930 --> 01:26:46.290
opinion regarding grading
a given dimension.

01:26:46.290 --> 01:26:47.690
So typically, what
you would have

01:26:47.690 --> 01:26:50.650
is that you first
give your input

01:26:50.650 --> 01:26:54.450
and then you handcraft a set
of guidelines with respect

01:26:54.450 --> 01:26:58.010
to each of the
dimensions of interest.

01:26:58.010 --> 01:27:02.810
And then you ask your
MLLM-as-a-Judge to output its

01:27:02.810 --> 01:27:05.710
decision in some given format.

01:27:05.710 --> 01:27:09.450
One that is popular is
JSON, because you can then

01:27:09.450 --> 01:27:13.010
parse scores and rationales
and anything else

01:27:13.010 --> 01:27:15.130
that you care about
in a structured way

01:27:15.130 --> 01:27:18.130
by just accessing fields.

01:27:18.130 --> 01:27:22.330
And one question
that comes to mind

01:27:22.330 --> 01:27:27.250
is how to ensure that the
rationales and the scores

01:27:27.250 --> 01:27:30.490
actually do make sense.

01:27:30.490 --> 01:27:33.810
So here comes the
most interesting part

01:27:33.810 --> 01:27:37.530
of the lecture, which is once
you have a problem, where you

01:27:37.530 --> 01:27:42.490
have a judge that you want
to use to grade your images,

01:27:42.490 --> 01:27:46.810
how are you going to make them
aligned with what you as a human

01:27:46.810 --> 01:27:48.850
find good?

01:27:48.850 --> 01:27:51.410
So this is the first
stage, where you

01:27:51.410 --> 01:27:55.867
would take in a true setting.

01:27:55.867 --> 01:27:57.450
You would take your
prompts, you would

01:27:57.450 --> 01:28:00.130
take your generated images,
and you would typically

01:28:00.130 --> 01:28:04.490
ask humans to grade them
alongside the rubrics

01:28:04.490 --> 01:28:05.030
of interest.

01:28:05.030 --> 01:28:07.970
So let's say, you have
perceptual quality and semantic

01:28:07.970 --> 01:28:12.610
consistency, you would first get
a sense of what is good versus

01:28:12.610 --> 01:28:15.290
bad from a human standpoint.

01:28:15.290 --> 01:28:21.730
And the second step would
be to handcraft rubrics that

01:28:21.730 --> 01:28:24.290
would match human intuition.

01:28:24.290 --> 01:28:31.600
So these days, handcraft
it's a bit of a strong word

01:28:31.600 --> 01:28:33.660
because you wouldn't
actually handcraft this.

01:28:33.660 --> 01:28:39.720
You would ask a model to
handcraft instructions

01:28:39.720 --> 01:28:43.680
that would be such that
the resulting ratings would

01:28:43.680 --> 01:28:46.320
match human intuition.

01:28:46.320 --> 01:28:50.560
And once you have that,
then you can further

01:28:50.560 --> 01:28:57.440
judge other prompts image
pairs with that trained judge.

01:28:57.440 --> 01:29:01.820
So this is a three stage
way of solving this problem.

01:29:01.820 --> 01:29:05.640
So first, just
asking your intuition

01:29:05.640 --> 01:29:11.920
about what is good, then tuning
the MLLMs rubrics about it,

01:29:11.920 --> 01:29:15.840
and then third one
would be directly asking

01:29:15.840 --> 01:29:17.860
the MLLM about new images.

01:29:20.800 --> 01:29:26.240
And so you might have
multiple settings,

01:29:26.240 --> 01:29:29.540
where you would ask for advice.

01:29:29.540 --> 01:29:32.440
So you have a pointwise
setting, where

01:29:32.440 --> 01:29:34.620
you have a given
image, a given prompt,

01:29:34.620 --> 01:29:37.560
and you want to know
if the result is good.

01:29:37.560 --> 01:29:43.240
You have the pairwise setting
that Afshin also mentioned,

01:29:43.240 --> 01:29:46.860
and you have another one
where you have a prompt,

01:29:46.860 --> 01:29:53.120
you have a list of images, and
you want the model to rank them.

01:29:53.120 --> 01:29:55.000
So going one by one.

01:29:55.000 --> 01:29:58.600
In practice, pointwise
is great when

01:29:58.600 --> 01:30:02.700
you want to do one-sided
evaluation of your images.

01:30:02.700 --> 01:30:07.040
So just to have a rough idea,
but also to debug what goes well

01:30:07.040 --> 01:30:09.740
versus doesn't go well,
because as you remember,

01:30:09.740 --> 01:30:14.960
you have this rationale side of
the MLLM-as-a-Judge output that

01:30:14.960 --> 01:30:20.860
can guide you towards
understanding loss modes

01:30:20.860 --> 01:30:22.720
of your model.

01:30:22.720 --> 01:30:26.000
So it can either lead to
you iterating on the image

01:30:26.000 --> 01:30:30.200
generation model, or coming back
to the calibration of the judge

01:30:30.200 --> 01:30:33.960
itself, because it might not
correspond to the intuition

01:30:33.960 --> 01:30:38.080
that you have in mind, so it's
great for doing both of them.

01:30:38.080 --> 01:30:40.880
Another setting is
the pairwise one,

01:30:40.880 --> 01:30:45.780
which is great when you want to
compare models with each other.

01:30:45.780 --> 01:30:49.480
So typically, if you have a
new iteration of the model

01:30:49.480 --> 01:30:52.780
and you want to know if it's
fine to replace it altogether,

01:30:52.780 --> 01:30:56.520
you would use this kind of mode.

01:30:56.520 --> 01:30:59.760
And then regarding
ranking, even though that's

01:30:59.760 --> 01:31:01.680
something that's
studied in papers,

01:31:01.680 --> 01:31:04.200
it's typically not
used in practice

01:31:04.200 --> 01:31:09.200
because the ordering
might be too sensitive,

01:31:09.200 --> 01:31:11.640
and so you have a
lot of variants.

01:31:11.640 --> 01:31:15.320
And oftentimes, the
ranking of several outputs

01:31:15.320 --> 01:31:19.160
might not be something that you
want to do as a one shot task,

01:31:19.160 --> 01:31:25.200
so it might not have as
broad applications as the two

01:31:25.200 --> 01:31:25.900
other ones.

01:31:28.680 --> 01:31:32.320
So I just want to spend a few
minutes to talk about best

01:31:32.320 --> 01:31:37.200
practices overall when you
deal with MLLM-as-a-Judge.

01:31:37.200 --> 01:31:41.160
So here, we saw with VIEScore
that you could decompose

01:31:41.160 --> 01:31:44.740
what you care about into two
dimensions, but in real life,

01:31:44.740 --> 01:31:52.440
you might have use cases, where
you have a task-specific rubric

01:31:52.440 --> 01:31:55.235
that is not general, but that
might be specific to what

01:31:55.235 --> 01:31:55.860
you care about.

01:31:55.860 --> 01:31:59.040
So you would typically
decompose and isolate

01:31:59.040 --> 01:32:03.000
that as a separate
metric and make

01:32:03.000 --> 01:32:07.900
the judging be done on all
these sets of criteria,

01:32:07.900 --> 01:32:10.280
including that
other one that would

01:32:10.280 --> 01:32:15.720
be designed to be atomic and
isolating what you care about.

01:32:15.720 --> 01:32:21.200
So another thing is that
empirically, you typically want

01:32:21.200 --> 01:32:25.360
your model to output the
rationale before outputting

01:32:25.360 --> 01:32:28.660
a score, because
in the LLM space,

01:32:28.660 --> 01:32:32.200
you have all this research
around concepts surrounding

01:32:32.200 --> 01:32:38.860
chains of thoughts and how
it improves the performance.

01:32:38.860 --> 01:32:42.120
And the intuition
for it is that even

01:32:42.120 --> 01:32:46.820
as a human, when you want to
form an opinion on something,

01:32:46.820 --> 01:32:50.700
it's always good to
enumerate facts about it.

01:32:50.700 --> 01:32:54.840
Just say out loud
what you have in mind

01:32:54.840 --> 01:32:58.160
when you do a piece of
judgment before actually

01:32:58.160 --> 01:33:00.320
doing the judgment.

01:33:00.320 --> 01:33:03.720
So this is a good practice.

01:33:03.720 --> 01:33:05.920
Something else is
that with LLMs, you

01:33:05.920 --> 01:33:12.000
have this temperature parameter
that controls how creative

01:33:12.000 --> 01:33:16.360
and how deterministic versus
stochastic your output gets.

01:33:16.360 --> 01:33:20.920
And typically, when
you use chatbots,

01:33:20.920 --> 01:33:22.700
these are not set to zero.

01:33:22.700 --> 01:33:25.200
So outputs are typically
non-deterministic

01:33:25.200 --> 01:33:29.000
because quality is
typically better when

01:33:29.000 --> 01:33:32.100
it's non-deterministic
for a range of tasks,

01:33:32.100 --> 01:33:37.640
but for very precise
tasks like judgments,

01:33:37.640 --> 01:33:42.520
where when you are grounded
on the input that you're given

01:33:42.520 --> 01:33:44.340
and the rubrics
that you're given,

01:33:44.340 --> 01:33:49.520
this is typically a use case
where it's fine to make it set

01:33:49.520 --> 01:33:53.280
to zero, and also because
you want determinism across

01:33:53.280 --> 01:33:56.720
your MLLM-as-a-Judge runs.

01:33:56.720 --> 01:34:00.840
Something else is that you
might see position bias, if you

01:34:00.840 --> 01:34:03.120
are in a pairwise
setting, so it's

01:34:03.120 --> 01:34:05.520
good to swap the ordering
of the two samples

01:34:05.520 --> 01:34:08.600
that you want to compare.

01:34:08.600 --> 01:34:12.760
And the last point is something
that we mentioned just before.

01:34:12.760 --> 01:34:15.880
We want to make sure it's
aligned with human judgment

01:34:15.880 --> 01:34:21.760
before actually going ahead
and then trusting it blindly.

01:34:21.760 --> 01:34:25.560
So I want to spend the
last 5 minutes talking

01:34:25.560 --> 01:34:28.400
about the main
benchmarks that cover

01:34:28.400 --> 01:34:31.320
some of the characteristics
that we care about

01:34:31.320 --> 01:34:33.360
on image generation.

01:34:33.360 --> 01:34:41.640
So on benchmarks, I
will go through points

01:34:41.640 --> 01:34:45.400
that cover overall areas
that we care about.

01:34:45.400 --> 01:34:48.400
So one is, let's say
you have a prompt.

01:34:48.400 --> 01:34:51.200
You want to make sure
that whatever is generated

01:34:51.200 --> 01:34:54.040
contains the objects
and some high level

01:34:54.040 --> 01:34:56.560
attributes that you
mentioned in your prompt.

01:34:56.560 --> 01:35:02.720
So this is where GenEval,
it's one possible benchmark,

01:35:02.720 --> 01:35:08.400
comes to mind, where you have
a six tasks of increasing

01:35:08.400 --> 01:35:12.080
difficulty that
talks about objects

01:35:12.080 --> 01:35:15.040
like counting of objects,
colors of objects,

01:35:15.040 --> 01:35:21.280
and relative position, as
well as attribution of colors.

01:35:21.280 --> 01:35:28.120
So the judges here are
object detection models

01:35:28.120 --> 01:35:30.200
that will come in
and then detect

01:35:30.200 --> 01:35:34.940
whether the right objects or
the right colors were generated,

01:35:34.940 --> 01:35:36.520
so this is one example.

01:35:36.520 --> 01:35:40.960
So you have a photo of a purple
backpack and a white umbrella,

01:35:40.960 --> 01:35:45.280
and you want to make sure that
both objects and colors are

01:35:45.280 --> 01:35:49.040
correctly attributed.

01:35:49.040 --> 01:35:51.040
And something else that
you care about that

01:35:51.040 --> 01:35:55.360
might touch on something
that you mentioned.

01:35:55.360 --> 01:35:58.780
So when you deal
with dense prompts,

01:35:58.780 --> 01:36:01.000
so typically, prompts
that are very detailed,

01:36:01.000 --> 01:36:03.920
you want to make sure
that all of the details

01:36:03.920 --> 01:36:05.880
are correctly rendered.

01:36:05.880 --> 01:36:10.520
And this is why these
DPG bench benchmark

01:36:10.520 --> 01:36:17.720
has a mechanism that looks a bit
like what we saw in TIFA, where

01:36:17.720 --> 01:36:22.360
you have this big chunk
of text that you decompose

01:36:22.360 --> 01:36:26.640
into a set of atomic
yes or no questions.

01:36:26.640 --> 01:36:28.920
And the interesting
thing about this paper

01:36:28.920 --> 01:36:33.680
is that it groups all of
these claims into a graph

01:36:33.680 --> 01:36:34.840
that is logical.

01:36:34.840 --> 01:36:37.320
So here, you have
a big chunk of text

01:36:37.320 --> 01:36:40.680
and it decomposes
into questions that

01:36:40.680 --> 01:36:44.320
are centered on
attributes, relationships,

01:36:44.320 --> 01:36:49.640
and spatial location.

01:36:49.640 --> 01:36:53.740
And let's say you want to
judge about a given attribute,

01:36:53.740 --> 01:36:58.800
so here, that's
long bunch of texts

01:36:58.800 --> 01:37:03.360
mentions like an empty space,
where an invisible man has

01:37:03.360 --> 01:37:04.640
this and that.

01:37:04.640 --> 01:37:08.000
So first, you need the
empty space to exist.

01:37:08.000 --> 01:37:13.940
And this benchmark looks at it
in a coarse to fine mindset.

01:37:13.940 --> 01:37:17.240
So if the prerequisite
doesn't exist,

01:37:17.240 --> 01:37:20.400
then you don't even bother
looking at the other conditions,

01:37:20.400 --> 01:37:23.840
so this is what makes this
benchmark interestingly

01:37:23.840 --> 01:37:25.600
efficient.

01:37:25.600 --> 01:37:30.180
And conversely, if one
precondition exists,

01:37:30.180 --> 01:37:34.280
then you move on to the
next pieces of the graph.

01:37:34.280 --> 01:37:37.080
So this is another area.

01:37:37.080 --> 01:37:41.480
So something else that we had
mentioned regarding capabilities

01:37:41.480 --> 01:37:44.900
that we were interested in
was OCR as a capability.

01:37:44.900 --> 01:37:46.860
And on the image
generation side,

01:37:46.860 --> 01:37:49.720
you might want to
generate images

01:37:49.720 --> 01:37:52.980
that have walls
of text in there,

01:37:52.980 --> 01:37:55.960
and you want to know how
well they are rendered.

01:37:55.960 --> 01:37:59.520
So there is a long
text bench that

01:37:59.520 --> 01:38:04.200
could be mentioned,
where you have scenarios,

01:38:04.200 --> 01:38:07.520
where displaying
text might be seen

01:38:07.520 --> 01:38:11.120
as interesting from an image
generation perspective.

01:38:11.120 --> 01:38:18.560
And the paper uses a VLM,
typically an OCR judge,

01:38:18.560 --> 01:38:22.840
that will try to render the
text in the generated image,

01:38:22.840 --> 01:38:26.040
and it will match to the
reference text that was

01:38:26.040 --> 01:38:28.680
used as a generation prompt.

01:38:28.680 --> 01:38:32.760
And if both match, then
we say that the text

01:38:32.760 --> 01:38:34.680
was correctly
generated, so it's one

01:38:34.680 --> 01:38:38.720
other interesting application.

01:38:38.720 --> 01:38:40.280
And then the last
one I will mention

01:38:40.280 --> 01:38:43.960
is on another image
generation mode

01:38:43.960 --> 01:38:46.320
that is of interest,
typically, when

01:38:46.320 --> 01:38:47.620
you want to generate an image.

01:38:47.620 --> 01:38:52.440
So if you want to operate
on top of a given image.

01:38:52.440 --> 01:38:58.560
So here, Grounded Edits Bench.

01:38:58.560 --> 01:39:01.640
So I put a question mark
because it's not explicitly

01:39:01.640 --> 01:39:07.580
spelled out in the paper,
but the abbreviation meaning,

01:39:07.580 --> 01:39:12.140
but the grounding part is on
the input image that is given.

01:39:12.140 --> 01:39:15.640
So you ask a task
about it, for example,

01:39:15.640 --> 01:39:18.980
replacing some background,
changing colors, and so on.

01:39:18.980 --> 01:39:27.760
So you have 11 such tasks, and
you use a MLLM-as-a-Judge model

01:39:27.760 --> 01:39:31.920
that operates on both perceptual
quality and semantic consistency

01:39:31.920 --> 01:39:33.420
to judge the output.

01:39:33.420 --> 01:39:40.680
So here's a figure from their
paper on what it looks like.

01:39:40.680 --> 01:39:44.400
And I just want to say one
last word about all of this.

01:39:44.400 --> 01:39:46.280
Sometimes you might
be overwhelmed

01:39:46.280 --> 01:39:47.450
with all these metrics.

01:39:47.450 --> 01:39:49.200
So we saw at the
beginning of the lecture,

01:39:49.200 --> 01:39:51.720
a bunch of quantitative ones.

01:39:51.720 --> 01:39:57.760
We saw MLLM-as-a-Judge ones that
are still imperfect because they

01:39:57.760 --> 01:40:01.200
depend on how well you tune
it, so there is no absolute way

01:40:01.200 --> 01:40:03.760
of knowing how
something performs.

01:40:03.760 --> 01:40:10.360
And you might be prone to only
relying on a few sample images

01:40:10.360 --> 01:40:16.760
as a way to who have an opinion
about a given image model,

01:40:16.760 --> 01:40:20.200
but I want to say that so
let's do the following thought

01:40:20.200 --> 01:40:21.680
experiment.

01:40:21.680 --> 01:40:25.120
If you consider
not the procedure

01:40:25.120 --> 01:40:28.800
of picking the next image that
is generated by your model,

01:40:28.800 --> 01:40:34.960
but the best of three, versus
the least well of three,

01:40:34.960 --> 01:40:38.200
then you see the
corresponding distributions

01:40:38.200 --> 01:40:39.680
are quite different.

01:40:39.680 --> 01:40:43.080
So from the same image
generation model,

01:40:43.080 --> 01:40:46.000
you could handcraft
different distributions just

01:40:46.000 --> 01:40:49.280
by the way you go
about sampling what

01:40:49.280 --> 01:40:51.600
you want to display to someone.

01:40:51.600 --> 01:40:53.760
So yeah, I just
want to put a caveat

01:40:53.760 --> 01:40:58.720
on when you see images doesn't
tell the whole story either.

01:40:58.720 --> 01:41:02.640
And with that, thank you
very much for coming here,

01:41:02.640 --> 01:41:03.893
despite the long weekend.

01:41:03.893 --> 01:41:05.060
I hope you have a great one.

01:41:05.060 --> 01:41:07.510
[APPLAUSE]

